window.COURSE=[{"id":1,"title":"Introduction to Linux","en":"Introduction to Linux","intro":"This chapter explains what Linux is, how the kernel relates to GNU and a distribution, and why the command line remains a core tool for administrators.","sections":[{"title":"Linux all around us","body":"Linux powers servers, clouds, transport systems, scientific systems, smartphones, and embedded devices. Linux skills apply to administration, development, networking, security, and data processing. Many commands follow UNIX conventions and remain useful across distributions."},{"title":"The kernel and the complete system","body":"Strictly speaking, Linux is a kernel: the central part of an OS, loaded at startup to manage resources. GNU supplies tools, compilers, editors, and interfaces. GNU/Linux combines the kernel with these tools; user applications complete the system."},{"title":"History and UNIX","body":"UNIX originated at Bell Labs, and the C language helped make it portable. Richard Stallman started GNU in 1983; Linus Torvalds started Linux in 1991. Linux is UNIX-like: similar design and interfaces do not imply UNIX certification. GNU stands for GNU’s Not Unix."},{"title":"Open source","body":"Open source provides access to source code and allows it to be studied and modified under its license. The community can share improvements and fixes. A proprietary license usually permits use of an executable without access to its source. Historically, Linux has been written mainly in C."},{"title":"Distributions","body":"A distribution combines the kernel, system tools, an installer, applications, and a package manager. Its contents and settings depend on its purpose: servers, desktops, education, or specialized tasks. The major historical families covered in this course are Debian, Red Hat, and Slackware."},{"title":"GUI and CLI","body":"A GUI uses windows, menus, and a pointer; a CLI accepts text commands through a shell. Linux can provide both interfaces. The CLI supports precise control, repeatable tasks, and automation; knowledge of common commands transfers between distributions."}],"commands":[],"lab":[],"pitfalls":["Linux as a kernel and Linux as a complete distribution refer to different levels of the system.","Having a GUI does not prevent you from using the CLI.","Open source does not necessarily mean free of charge or free of bugs."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"What does Linux mean in the strictest technical sense?","options":["An operating system kernel","Any open-source program","Only a graphical desktop","A text editor standard"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The kernel manages resources; the complete user-facing system is called a distribution.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which component continues running after startup and manages system resources?","options":["Installation image","Kernel","Source archive","Documentation"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The kernel loads at startup and serves running processes and hardware.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does GNU contribute to GNU/Linux?","options":["Only a logo","Only printer drivers","Tools, editors, compilers, and other system programs","Mandatory certification of every computer"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"GNU tools helped turn the kernel into a usable operating system.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Who started developing the Linux kernel?","options":["Richard Stallman","Linus Torvalds","Dennis Ritchie","Bill Gates"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Linus Torvalds started Linux as his own kernel project.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"In which year did the Linux project begin? Enter the year.","answer":["1991"],"explanation":"The lecture dates the start of the Linux project to 1991.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which project did Richard Stallman start in 1983?","options":["Android","Fedora","GNU","Apache"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"GNU developed free software tools and planned a free UNIX-like operating system.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is Linux described as UNIX-like in this chapter?","options":["It has a similar design without the UNIX certification discussed in the chapter","It can only run on a UNIX server","It is a text editor included in UNIX","It contains no kernel"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Similarity to UNIX does not automatically grant the right to use the certified UNIX name.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does the recursive acronym GNU stand for?","options":["General Network Utility","Global New UNIX","GNU’s Not Unix","Graphical Network User"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The first word in the expansion contains the acronym GNU itself.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which feature of C helped UNIX spread?","options":["Dependence on a single computer model","Portability between hardware platforms","Prohibition of compilation","A mandatory graphical interface"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"C helped reduce the OS’s dependence on specific hardware.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What must be added to a kernel to create a familiar user system?","options":["Only a company name","System tools and applications","Only desktop wallpaper","Only a new license"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The kernel alone does not supply the full set of user programs.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 components of a typical distribution.","options":["The Linux kernel","An installer and a package manager","A mandatory subscription to one cloud service","A mandatory absence of applications"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A distribution supplies the kernel and tools for installing and maintaining software.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A company chooses Linux for a specialized scientific computer. What follows from the lecture?","options":["All distributions are designed only for web browsing","A distribution can be chosen to suit the task","The kernel must be removed","Scientific applications are incompatible with Linux by definition"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Distributions differ in their intended uses and software selections.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tool makes adding and removing software convenient after installing a distribution?","options":["Package manager","Only the boot loader","Desktop wallpaper","Only the text cursor"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A package manager maintains installed software.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 open-source characteristics described in the lecture.","options":["Source code can be obtained","Code can be modified under the license","The author must provide round-the-clock support","All bugs disappear automatically"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Source access allows study and modification but does not guarantee support or the absence of bugs.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A user receives an executable without source code. Is that enough to call the program open source?","options":["Yes, every executable is open source","Yes, if the file is small","No, source access and license rights matter","No, open source can exist only on paper"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"An executable and source code are different forms of a program.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which example uses a CLI?","options":["Typing a text command in a terminal","Dragging a window with the mouse","Choosing a color from a graphical menu","Clicking an application icon on the desktop"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A CLI involves entering commands as text.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which CLI advantage is especially useful when repeating a task on many systems?","options":["Automation through commands and scripts","Prohibiting remote access","Mandatory manual dragging of windows","Having no parameters at all"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Command sequences can be saved and repeated in scripts.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An administrator switches distributions. Why are the commands already learned still useful?","options":["All graphical interfaces must be identical","Many basic commands and conventions are shared","Every distribution uses the Windows API instead of Linux","A CLI exists in only one distribution"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The shared UNIX-like foundation reduces dependence on a particular desktop.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which pair correctly matches an interface to its interaction method?","options":["GUI: windows and menus; CLI: text commands","GUI: binary instructions only; CLI: mouse only","GUI: kernel; CLI: hardware memory","GUI: license; CLI: distribution"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"These interfaces differ in how users interact with them; neither is a kernel or a license.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A colleague claims, “Linux is used exclusively on desktop PCs.” Which lecture example disproves this?","options":["Servers and embedded devices","Only changing wallpaper","Only a desktop keyboard","Only an office document"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Linux is used in servers, clouds, and specialized devices.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 1 - Introduction to Linux (1).txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Kg9Zm93wv5swTaufw3D6HhIkH9f97ecZ/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":2,"title":"Operating Systems and Distributions","en":"Operating Systems","intro":"Choosing an OS starts with the computer’s role, required applications, support, and stability needs. Distinguish distribution families, release cycles, and support lifetimes.","sections":[{"title":"What an OS does","body":"An OS manages hardware and software resources, allocates processor time among tasks, and provides common services to applications. A server serves remote clients; a workstation focuses on direct user interaction. One machine can have several roles."},{"title":"Selection criteria","body":"Consider the role, required applications, hardware and library compatibility, team skills, purchase and maintenance costs, lifespan, and interface. A free distribution download does not eliminate support and maintenance costs."},{"title":"Releases and maintenance","body":"A release cycle describes how frequently new versions appear; a maintenance or life cycle is the period of fixes and support. A beta contains new changes that are still being tested. A stable release is preferable for predictable operation. LTS means long-term support; backward compatibility means being able to run software from earlier versions."},{"title":"Windows, macOS, and Linux","body":"Windows has its own non-UNIX code base, desktop and server versions, and automation tools. macOS uses BSD components and UNIX heritage and is closely tied to Apple hardware. Linux comes in many distributions. Version numbers described as current in the lecture reflect when it was written."},{"title":"Distribution families","body":"Red Hat is associated with RPM; RHEL targets enterprises, while Fedora offers faster updates. The lecture describes CentOS and Scientific Linux as RHEL derivatives in their historical context. SUSE historically grew out of Slackware and has enterprise and community variants. Debian uses .deb packages; Ubuntu derives from Debian, and the main Mint edition derives from Ubuntu."},{"title":"Specialized variants","body":"Android uses the Linux kernel but has a different user environment from conventional GNU/Linux: sharing a kernel does not guarantee application compatibility. Raspbian is presented in the lecture as a system for Raspberry Pi. Linux From Scratch is an educational project for building your own system from source."},{"title":"Embedded systems and clouds","body":"An embedded system performs a specific task on specialized hardware, such as a router or sensor. IoT connects such devices through networks. Virtualization allows several guest operating systems to share a physical computer; clouds provide remote resources and reduce the need to buy all infrastructure in advance."},{"title":"Terminal and shell","body":"A terminal accepts input and displays output; a shell interprets commands. A GUI is convenient for interactive work, while a server can use a CLI to conserve resources. MOTD is a message displayed after login; it is not a command prompt."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"w","description":"Show logged-in users and information about their sessions."}],"lab":[],"pitfalls":["A release cycle and a support lifetime are different concepts.","Android does not necessarily run packages intended for conventional desktop GNU/Linux.","Historical Windows, CentOS, and Android versions and support dates in the lecture should not be treated as a current product catalog."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Which task is an operating system’s responsibility?","options":["Allocating resources among programs","Writing all application source code","Choosing the user’s thesis topic","Always displaying a GUI on every server"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The OS manages memory, the processor, devices, and program execution.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A computer mainly provides files to remote users. What role does it perform?","options":["Server","Only a graphics editor","Compiler","License"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Its role is defined by serving clients, not by the appearance of its case.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is a server often installed without a graphical environment?","options":["To reduce resources spent on an unnecessary interface","Because Linux does not support GUIs","Because a CLI prohibits networking","To remove the ability to administer it"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Remote administration often does not require a GUI.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does release cycle mean?","options":["The timing and pattern of new version releases","The duration of one login session","The number of files in an archive","A user password’s validity period"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Release frequency is not the same as the support lifetime of an individual release.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An organization checks the last date on which fixes will be issued for its installed version. What is it investigating?","options":["Maintenance cycle","Desktop size","Task-switching frequency","Home directory name"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The maintenance cycle defines how long a version is supported.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What best describes a beta release?","options":["A version with new features that are still being tested","Always a final version without bugs","Another spelling of root","A version only for printers"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A beta can be useful for testing but carries more uncertainty.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Predictability matters more than the newest features on a critical server. Which option meets this requirement?","options":["A stable release with a suitable support period","Any unstable release without assessing risks","Mandatory upgrades to every beta","Rejecting security fixes"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A tested release and ongoing support reduce operational risks.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"What does LTS stand for?","answer":["long-term support","long term support","Long-term support","Long-term Support","Long-Term Support","Long Term Support","LONG-TERM SUPPORT","LONG TERM SUPPORT"],"explanation":"LTS means long-term support for a release.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An old application still works after an OS upgrade. What is this property called?","options":["Backward compatibility","Compression","Globbing","Multimedia"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Backward compatibility preserves the ability to use software from earlier versions.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 factors that genuinely matter when selecting a distribution for a company.","options":["Support for the required application","The maintenance team’s skills","Only the logo color","Only the length of the name"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Supported applications and team expertise directly affect operation.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which system in the lecture has BSD foundations and UNIX certification?","options":["macOS","MS-DOS","Any system built with Linux From Scratch","Any Android firmware"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The OS comparison highlights macOS’s UNIX heritage and certification.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which package format is associated with Debian?","options":[".deb",".jpg",".wav",".rpm"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Debian and its derivatives use .deb packages.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which family is directly associated with RPM in the lecture?","options":["Red Hat","Only macOS","Only iOS","GNU as a separate package family"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"RPM originated in the Red Hat ecosystem.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which distribution is derived from Debian?","options":["Ubuntu","RHEL","SUSE as described in the chapter","Windows Server"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Ubuntu uses Debian as its historical foundation.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What role does the lecture associate with Fedora in the Red Hat ecosystem?","options":["Faster updates and new technologies","Only a keyboard driver","Only a paid document archive","A complete absence of a package manager"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Fedora develops new capabilities faster than the enterprise line.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does embedded mean in an OS context?","options":["A system inside a device designed for specific tasks","Any OS with a large monitor","A file moved to the trash","Only a virtual machine"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Examples include sensors, household devices, and network equipment.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A student wants to learn how to build Linux from source step by step. Which lecture project suits this purpose?","options":["Linux From Scratch","Thunderbird","GIMP","Sendmail"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"LFS is an educational guide to building your own system.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does an application from Google Play not automatically become a standard Ubuntu package?","options":["User environments and APIs differ despite sharing the Linux kernel","Android has no kernel at all","Ubuntu cannot run programs","All programs must be written for one screen"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A shared kernel does not imply identical libraries or application formats.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does virtualization allow?","options":["Running several guest operating systems on one physical host","Making physical memory infinite","Eliminating the need for a processor","Guaranteeing zero costs"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"One host’s resources are divided among virtual machines.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 correct statements distinguishing a terminal from a shell.","options":["A terminal accepts input and displays text","A shell interprets commands","A terminal is always the kernel","A shell must be a monitor"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The terminal provides interaction, while the shell parses and runs commands.","difficulty":"basic"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 2 - Operating Systems.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gfYom-TqH4e9lTDswQGqJQsOe9AJxY2Y/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":3,"title":"Applications and Working in Linux","en":"Working in Linux","intro":"Learn the main server and desktop applications, package systems, development languages, and basic principles of security, cloud computing, and virtualization.","sections":[{"title":"Desktop and console","body":"A CLI can be opened in a graphical terminal or a separate virtual console. A terminal and a shell are different components. Applications request resources through OS interfaces; the kernel tracks processes and switches tasks. A single program may consist of several processes."},{"title":"Web, data, and private clouds","body":"Apache HTTPD and NGINX serve HTTP/HTTPS requests. A static page is sent as a ready-made file; an application generates a dynamic page, with WordPress as one example. MariaDB is a fork of MySQL; PostgreSQL and Firebird are also database management systems. SQL is used to query data. ownCloud and Nextcloud support file storage, synchronization, and sharing on private infrastructure."},{"title":"Email","body":"An MTA transfers email between systems; examples include Sendmail and Postfix. An MDA places a message in a local mailbox. POP/IMAP servers such as Dovecot and Cyrus give clients access to mail. Thunderbird, Evolution, and KMail are user-facing clients, not replacements for every server component."},{"title":"Files and network services","body":"Samba enables interoperability with Windows shares; NFS provides typical UNIX/Linux network file access; Netatalk is associated with Apple file sharing. DNS maps names and addresses; BIND is a DNS server. DHCP assigns network settings, including an IP address. LDAP provides access to hierarchical directory information; OpenLDAP implements this protocol."},{"title":"Desktop applications","body":"LibreOffice includes Writer for documents, Calc for spreadsheets, and presentation tools; the suite originated from OpenOffice. GIMP edits raster images, Blender handles 3D work, and Audacity handles audio. Firefox and Chrome are available on Linux; distinguish the open-source Chromium project from the Chrome product."},{"title":"Shells and editors","body":"Bash belongs to the Bourne shell family; tcsh belongs to the C shell family. Other shells include ksh and zsh. Vi/Vim and Emacs are powerful editors; Nano is simpler for small edits. Bash supports repeating tasks, using variables, and creating scripts."},{"title":"Packages and repositories","body":"A package manager tracks files, versions, and dependencies. Debian/Ubuntu use .deb packages, the low-level dpkg tool, and APT tools such as apt-get in the lecture. Red Hat/Fedora use .rpm packages, rpm, and repository tools such as yum in the lecture. SUSE uses zypper. Changing installed system packages usually requires administrative privileges; looking up information is generally available to regular users."},{"title":"Languages and libraries","body":"C compiles to machine code; C++ adds object-oriented features. Java usually compiles to bytecode for the JVM; JavaScript is a different language widely used on the web. Perl is useful for text processing and automation, PHP for server-side web development, and Ruby/Rails and Python/Django for applications. Libraries provide reusable functionality: OpenSSL for cryptography, ImageMagick for images, and the C library for basic operations."},{"title":"Security and privacy","body":"Cookies store website state and can be used for tracking. Private browsing reduces local traces of a session but does not guarantee complete anonymity. Use unique passwords, a password manager, a second authentication factor, and updates. Root is a privileged account. A firewall filters network traffic; UFW/Gufw simplify rule management."},{"title":"HTTPS, VPN, and Tor","body":"HTTPS protects the connection between a browser and a web server; a VPN creates a protected tunnel between endpoints. Tor forwards traffic through a network of nodes to reduce the connection between requests and the original address. These mechanisms serve different purposes and do not remove the need to handle passwords and data carefully."},{"title":"Cloud models","body":"A public cloud serves many customers; a private cloud serves one organization; a community cloud serves organizations with shared requirements; a hybrid cloud combines multiple clouds. Linux suits cloud environments because of its modularity, automation, scalability, and available tools."},{"title":"Virtualization and containers","body":"The host is the physical computer, a guest is a guest OS, and a hypervisor allocates resources among VMs. A bare-metal hypervisor runs directly on the hardware. Containers isolate applications using the host kernel; Docker and Kubernetes are associated with packaging and managing container workloads. A container should not be treated as a separate, complete guest OS."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"zypper in package","description":"Install a package and its dependencies on systems using zypper; appropriate privileges are required."},{"cmd":"dpkg","description":"Low-level Debian package management."},{"cmd":"rpm","description":"Low-level RPM package management."}],"lab":[],"pitfalls":["An MTA, an MDA, and an email client perform different tasks.","Java and JavaScript are different languages.","HTTPS and private browsing do not make a user completely anonymous.","Containers use the host kernel, whereas virtual machines have a guest OS.","References to older tools and versions in the lecture reflect the course context."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"A browser opens a dynamic page containing student data. How is its content usually produced?","options":["An application generates a response, querying a database if needed","The browser must read only an unchanging file","DNS draws the student table","DHCP executes the application’s SQL query"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A dynamic page is generated by application logic based on a request and data.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 web servers.","options":["Apache HTTPD","NGINX","Audacity","GIMP"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Apache HTTPD and NGINX handle web requests.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which product is a database management system?","options":["MariaDB","Thunderbird","Blender","Bash"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"MariaDB stores data and handles queries; the other tools have different roles.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which query language is named in the database section? Enter its abbreviation.","answer":["SQL","sql","Sql"],"explanation":"Structured Query Language is used to work with data in relational database management systems.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"An organization needs its own file synchronization service. Choose 2 suitable projects from the lecture.","options":["ownCloud","Nextcloud","Nano","Postfix"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Both projects provide private file storage, synchronization, and sharing.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does an MTA do?","options":["Transfers email messages between systems","Edits images","Assigns IP addresses","Displays only a local calendar"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"MTA stands for Mail Transfer Agent; examples include Postfix and Sendmail.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which component in the delivery chain saves a message in the user’s local mailbox?","options":["MDA","DNS","GIMP","C compiler"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A Mail Delivery Agent handles local message delivery.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which application is a desktop email client?","options":["Thunderbird","Postfix","BIND","MariaDB"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Thunderbird is a client, whereas Postfix transfers messages between systems.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need to make a Linux shared resource available to Windows clients. Which option should you choose?","options":["Samba","Audacity","Ruby","GNU Info"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Samba provides compatibility with Windows file sharing.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which protocol is typically used to mount network file resources on UNIX/Linux?","options":["NFS","SMTP","SQL","HTML"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"NFS makes a remote file system accessible through the local directory hierarchy.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A computer is automatically assigned an available IP address. Which service does this?","options":["DHCP","DNS","MDA","HTTPD"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"DHCP assigns an address and other network settings; DNS resolves names.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An application translates a server name into an IP address. Which service is needed?","options":["DNS","Nano","ZIP","Calc"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"DNS stores information about names and associated network resources.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A directory stores organizational departments and users in a tree structure. Which protocol fits this description?","options":["LDAP","JPEG","SSH as an image format","RPM"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"LDAP is designed to access hierarchical directory data.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 correct pairings.","options":["Audacity: audio processing","Blender: 3D graphics","GIMP: assigning IP addresses","LibreOffice Calc: transferring email between servers"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Creative tools specialize in different areas such as audio, 3D, and 2D graphics.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which LibreOffice application is primarily intended for spreadsheets and formulas?","options":["Calc","Writer","Postfix","Dovecot"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Calc handles spreadsheets; Writer handles text documents.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tool is a shell?","options":["Bash","GIMP","MariaDB","Firefox"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A shell accepts commands and provides a scripting language.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need to quickly edit a small text file in the console. Which simple editor is mentioned in the lecture?","options":["Nano","NGINX","BIND","Samba"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Nano is a console text editor with a simple interface.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which pair correctly matches a low-level tool to its package format?","options":["dpkg — .deb","dpkg — .rpm","rpm — .docx","zypper — .jpg"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"dpkg works with Debian packages.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does a package manager check dependencies?","options":["An application may require other packages and libraries","Every package must contain a browser","A dependency means only the icon color","To prohibit updates"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An installed program may fail to run without its required libraries.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which operation is usually available without root privileges?","options":["Searching for package information","Removing a system package","Updating all system libraries","Writing a package into a system directory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Looking up metadata usually does not change the system’s state.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What executes Java bytecode?","options":["JVM","Only Bash without additional components","Only GIMP","A DNS server"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The Java Virtual Machine executes bytecode on a supported platform.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which library is associated with cryptography?","options":["OpenSSL","LibreOffice Calc","NFS","Thunderbird"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"OpenSSL provides cryptographic functions to other programs.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A user opens a private browser window. Which conclusion is correct?","options":["It does not guarantee anonymity from websites and network participants","All websites stop receiving the user’s requests","The system automatically gains root privileges","All passwords become unique"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Private browsing primarily limits local storage of session data.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 measures that improve account security.","options":["Unique passwords","Two-factor authentication","One password for every website","Sharing a password in a public chat"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Different passwords limit the impact of a breach; a second factor adds an independent check.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Cloud infrastructure is dedicated exclusively to one organization’s needs. This is a…","options":["Private cloud","Public cloud by definition","Local text file only","System that cannot be a cloud"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A private cloud serves one organization and may be hosted by an external provider.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Two organizations with shared requirements jointly use a separate cloud. Which model best fits?","options":["Community cloud","Always a hybrid cloud in every case","A separate shell alias","Only a public cloud"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A community cloud serves a group of organizations with shared goals.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What distinguishes a hypervisor from a guest application?","options":["It manages virtual machine resources","It is only a spreadsheet","It must run inside a browser","It replaces all hardware"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A hypervisor supports the operation of multiple guest systems.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 correct statements about virtualization.","options":["One host can run several guest operating systems","Guests can use different operating systems","Every guest must have a separate physical monitor","Virtualization makes RAM infinite"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"VMs share the host’s finite resources; guest operating systems do not have to match.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement most accurately describes a container?","options":["It isolates an application using the host kernel","It must contain a separate physical CPU core","It always equals a new complete guest OS","It is unrelated to the operating system"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Container isolation differs from a virtual machine with its own guest kernel.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What combines several separate public, private, or community clouds?","options":["Hybrid cloud","One password","Only a file extension","Shell history"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A hybrid model connects different cloud infrastructures.","difficulty":"basic"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 3 - Working in Linux.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1neYfQprUtL2vsBIG25UXfaeIV4B0amzt/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":4,"title":"Open Source Software and Licensing","en":"Open Source Software and Licensing","intro":"This chapter covers software freedoms, access to source code, and distribution conditions. Distinguish price, copyright, and licensing: they are not the same thing.","sections":[{"title":"Source and executable code","body":"Developers read and modify source code. A compiler translates it into executable instructions; an interpreter executes script instructions. Access to a binary alone does not make software open source. An open development process helps people fix bugs together and study program behavior."},{"title":"Standards and portability","body":"UNIX, C, and standardized interfaces helped applications move between systems. POSIX defines common interfaces and behavior; standards compliance supports compatibility regardless of whether the code is open."},{"title":"Ownership, payment, and licenses","body":"Copyright, price, and terms of use are three separate things. A license specifies which actions are allowed. A free download does not by itself grant permission to modify or distribute the code. Open-source software can be sold and commercially supported."},{"title":"FSF and copyleft","body":"Richard Stallman founded the Free Software Foundation; free here means freedom to study, use, modify, and distribute software. Copyleft preserves these freedoms for distributed derivative works. GPL is a family of copyleft licenses; the Linux kernel uses GPLv2. This does not mean “every private modification must immediately be published”: the distribution context matters. The lecture also mentions LGPL for libraries. Differences between GPLv2 and GPLv3 are illustrated by Tivoization: source is available, but a device prevents modified builds from running; GPLv3 addresses such restrictions separately."},{"title":"OSI and permissive licenses","body":"The Open Source Initiative evaluates licenses against open-source criteria. BSD and MIT are permissive licenses: they do not impose general copyleft on all derivative works, but retain their own conditions, such as copyright notices. FOSS combines free and open-source software; FLOSS adds libre to emphasize freedom rather than zero price."},{"title":"Creative Commons","body":"CC primarily applies to creative content. BY requires attribution; SA requires distributing adaptations under the same terms; NC restricts commercial use; ND prohibits distributing derivative works without additional permission. Combinations include CC BY, BY-SA, BY-ND, BY-NC, BY-NC-SA, and BY-NC-ND. CC0 aims to waive rights to the greatest extent possible."},{"title":"Open-source business models","body":"Companies earn money through support, warranties, integration, training, hardware, and additional products. Organizations also support development when they need a project for their own systems. The ability to sell software does not remove its distribution license conditions."}],"commands":[],"lab":[],"pitfalls":["Free software is about freedoms, not a necessarily zero price.","Copyleft is not the same as the public domain.","Open source does not mean an absence of copyright.","BY, SA, NC, and ND are separate conditions: check the whole combination.","The lecture simplifies some legal details; the questions test basic course concepts."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Which three concepts must be distinguished when discussing a software purchase?","options":["Rights ownership, payment, and licensing","CPU, RAM, and monitor","Only brand, size, and color","Directory, file, and link"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Price does not automatically determine modification and distribution rights.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A developer allows a proprietary program to be downloaded free of charge. Which conclusion is correct?","options":["This does not necessarily make it open source","Its source automatically entered the public domain","The user automatically acquired copyright","The GPL applies without the author’s agreement"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A zero price and an open-source license are independent properties.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does free mean in the FSF philosophy?","options":["User freedoms","Necessarily zero cost","No distribution conditions whatsoever","Necessarily no support"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The FSF emphasizes the freedom to use, study, modify, and distribute software.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which organization is associated with creating the GPL family?","options":["FSF","Mozilla as the author of every GPL","ISC as the author of every GPL","Xerox PARC"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The Free Software Foundation develops the GPL and related licenses.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the main idea of copyleft?","options":["Preserving freedoms and license terms when derivative works are distributed","Prohibiting study of the code","Abolishing all copyright","Allowing only private reading of documentation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Copyleft uses license conditions to preserve the openness of distributed derivative works.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which GPL version does the lecture associate with the Linux kernel?","options":["GPLv2","CC BY-ND","Only BSD 2-Clause","None"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The course presents the Linux kernel as a GPLv2 project.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the role of the Open Source Initiative?","options":["Evaluating licenses against open source principles","Assigning IP addresses","Maintaining every Linux package","Writing all proprietary EULAs"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The OSI maintains the Open Source Definition and approves licenses that meet it.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 permissive licenses mentioned in the lecture.","options":["MIT","BSD","GPL as an example of strong copyleft","CC BY-NC-ND as a software license"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"MIT and BSD are generally classified as permissive free software licenses.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement about permissive licenses is most accurate?","options":["They may allow code to be included in a proprietary product if their conditions are met","They contain no conditions at all","They always prohibit selling software","They require publication of all unrelated company code"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Permissiveness does not remove requirements to retain notices or meet other specific conditions.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is the word libre included in FLOSS?","options":["To distinguish freedom from zero price","To prohibit Linux on servers","To identify a file format","To replace software with server"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Libre removes the ambiguity of the English word free.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is Creative Commons primarily intended for?","options":["Creative content such as text, illustrations, and similar works","Only kernel drivers","Only routing tables","Only hardware addresses"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"CC applies permissions for reuse to content outside ordinary software licensing.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which CC condition requires attribution to the author? Enter two letters.","answer":["BY","by","By"],"explanation":"Attribution is represented by BY.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An adaptation must be distributed under the same license terms. Which CC element expresses this?","options":["SA","NC","ND","RPM"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"ShareAlike preserves license terms when a derivative work is distributed.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does NC mean in CC BY-NC?","options":["NonCommercial","NoCopyright","NewCompiler","NetworkControl"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"NC restricts commercial use.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An author permits distribution of an unchanged work, but not adaptations without permission. Which element expresses this?","options":["ND","SA","BY as the only restriction","LTS"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"NoDerivatives concerns distribution of modified versions.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"In CC BY-NC-SA, choose 2 explicit conditions besides attribution.","options":["Restriction of commercial use","Preserving terms for distributed adaptations","A complete prohibition of all adaptations","Mandatory compilation of the material"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"NC and SA express different restrictions; ND is absent from this combination.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does CC0 represent in the course comparison?","options":["A rights-waiver tool resembling the public domain","A special license only for root","A prohibition of all copying","An archive with zero compression"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"CC0 aims to remove copyright and related-rights restrictions to the greatest extent possible.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Can a business earn money by supporting open-source software?","options":["Yes, for example through support and integration","No, the word free prohibits all income","Only if it hides all source code","Only if it removes the license"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A commercial model can sell services around an open-source product.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What benefit do standard APIs, such as POSIX interfaces, provide?","options":["They improve application portability and compatibility","They guarantee identical prices","They cancel every license","They prohibit different operating systems"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Standards define common interfaces independently of the distribution model.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A colleague says, “Copyleft means there is no copyright.” Why is this incorrect?","options":["Copyleft relies on copyright and license conditions","Copyleft is only a compression algorithm","Copyleft applies only to hardware","Copyright exists only for proprietary programs"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A license uses the author’s rights to establish freedoms and obligations.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 4 - Open Source Software and Licensing.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YVAqjr6IXqlBdqTF9olCkSTtbhIGRwxH/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":5,"title":"Command Line Skills","en":"Command Line Skills","intro":"Learn to read a command as “name + options + arguments” and understand Bash, variables, quoting, and conditional execution. These skills underpin most practical questions.","sections":[{"title":"Shell and prompt","body":"Bash interprets commands and supports history, scripts, functions, variables, and aliases. The prompt sysadmin@localhost:~$ usually shows the user, hostname, and current directory. Prompts are customizable, so their appearance is not guaranteed."},{"title":"Commands, options, and arguments","body":"The general form is command [options] [arguments]. In ls -l /home, ls is the command, -l is an option, and /home is an argument. Short options can often be combined: ls -lr equals ls -l -r. Long options usually begin with --. Case matters."},{"title":"History","body":"history shows a numbered list; history 5 shows the last five entries. The up and down arrow keys select commands for editing. !n repeats entry n; !-n selects the nth entry from the end; !! repeats the last command; !ls repeats the last command starting with ls. HISTSIZE sets the size of the Bash history list."},{"title":"Variables and the environment","body":"Assign a variable with name=value, without spaces around =. Read its value as $name; echo prints it. export makes a variable available to child processes; env displays the environment; unset removes a variable. Export does not automatically change the environment of independent shells that are already running, nor does it permanently save a value by itself."},{"title":"Command lookup and PATH","body":"PATH contains colon-separated directories used to search for executable commands. Their order matters. which searches PATH for an external executable; a full path refers to it explicitly. type recognizes built-ins, aliases, and functions; type -a displays all matches found."},{"title":"Command types","body":"A built-in, such as cd, is executed by the shell itself. An external command is a separate executable file. An alias replaces a short name with a specified command line: alias ll='ls -l'. A function groups a sequence of commands. Settings created only in the current shell usually disappear when it closes."},{"title":"Quoting and escaping","body":"Single quotes preserve text literally. Double quotes disable globbing but allow $variable and $(command) substitution. A backslash escapes the next special character. Backticks and $(...) substitute command output. An unclosed quote makes the shell wait for more input."},{"title":"Sequences and conditions","body":"A ; B runs B after A regardless of the result. A && B runs B only if A succeeds; A || B runs B only if A fails. true always succeeds; false always fails. An absence of visible output does not imply failure."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"whoami","description":"Show the current username."},{"cmd":"uname -n","description":"Show the network node name; equivalent to --nodename."},{"cmd":"pwd","description":"Show the current directory."},{"cmd":"history 5","description":"Show the last five history entries."},{"cmd":"type -a echo","description":"Show built-in and external implementations of echo, if available."},{"cmd":"which date","description":"Find an external date executable through PATH."},{"cmd":"export course=Linux","description":"Create an exported variable."},{"cmd":"echo \"$course\"","description":"Print the variable’s value."},{"cmd":"alias ll='ls -l'","description":"Create an abbreviation for a long-format listing."},{"cmd":"false || echo retry","description":"Run echo after a command fails."}],"lab":[{"task":"Identify the current user, hostname, and working directory.","answer":"whoami\nuname -n\npwd","explanation":"These are three different environment properties; the prompt may not show all of them."},{"task":"Display the last five commands and repeat the most recent entry without retyping it.","answer":"history 5\n!!","explanation":"history displays entries; !! executes the last one, so using it immediately after history repeats history itself."},{"task":"Create a variable named course with the value Linux and make it available to child processes.","answer":"course=Linux\nexport course\necho \"$course\"","explanation":"Without export, this is a current-shell variable; export adds it to the environment of future child processes."},{"task":"Compare literal text with a substituted variable value.","answer":"echo '$HOME'\necho \"$HOME\"","explanation":"The first command prints the literal text $HOME; the second prints the value of HOME."},{"task":"Predict the result of echo Start && false && echo End.","answer":"Only Start will be printed.","explanation":"After false, the condition for the final command after && is not met."},{"task":"Create a short name for a detailed listing and check its type.","answer":"alias ll='ls -l'\ntype ll","explanation":"type reports an alias; this is not a new external executable."}],"pitfalls":["Spaces in name=value change how the command is parsed.","export does not mean “change the variable in all terminals that are already open.”","Double quotes do not block variable substitution.","; does not check whether the previous command succeeded.","which and type can report different results for the same command."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"In ls -l /var, which argument specifies the object to act on?","options":["/var","ls","-l","The entire line is one option"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The path /var specifies a directory; -l changes the output format.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which ls command is equivalent to ls -l -r?","options":["ls -lr","ls --lr","ls /lr","ls -L -R"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The short options -l and -r can be combined; their case must not be changed.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does ~ mean in a typical prompt such as anna@pc:~$?","options":["anna’s home directory","The file system root","Always /tmp","A package name"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The tilde is shorthand for the current user’s home directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which assignment correctly creates a variable named label?","options":["label=Linux","label = Linux","$label=Linux","set label Linux as Bash assignment syntax"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A normal Bash assignment has no spaces around =.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After item=book, what does echo \"$item\" print?","options":["book","$item","item","Nothing, because echo cannot read variables"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Within double quotes, Bash substitutes the variable’s value.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After item=book, what does echo '$item' print?","options":["$item","book","item=book","A filename error"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Single quotes prevent substitution.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is export used for?","options":["Passing a variable into the environment of future child processes","Saving all variables permanently","Changing values in all independent terminals","Converting text into an archive file"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Export affects the environment inherited by child processes.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command removes the variable item from the current shell?","options":["unset item","rm $item","delete item","export -all item"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"unset operates on shell variables; rm deletes files.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which character separates directories in PATH? Enter the character.","answer":[":"],"explanation":"A colon separates one search directory from the next.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"PATH=/tools:/usr/bin. An executable named report exists in both directories, with no alias or function of that name. Which path is found first?","options":["/tools/report","/usr/bin/report","Both run in sequence","The choice is always random"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"PATH directories are searched in the listed order.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why might type echo report a built-in while which echo reports /bin/echo?","options":["echo can have both built-in and external implementations","One of the tools must be broken","which always displays the home directory","type never works with commands"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"type recognizes shell entities; which normally searches PATH for an external file.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you inspect all available versions of the echo command?","options":["type -a echo","echo -a type","history echo","env echo -a"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The -a option makes type display all definitions or locations it finds.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which example creates an alias for ls -l?","options":["alias ll='ls -l'","alias=ll ls -l","ll alias ls -l","export ll ls -l"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An alias is defined as name='command line'.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What distinguishes a shell function from a simple command-name abbreviation?","options":["It can combine several commands and logic","It must be stored in the kernel","It cannot be called by name","It always changes the file system"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Functions let you invoke a group of actions repeatedly.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The current directory contains a.txt and b.txt. What does echo \"*.txt\" print?","options":["*.txt","a.txt b.txt","Only a.txt","The contents of both files"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Double quotes suppress globbing.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 types of substitution that normally remain active inside Bash double quotes.","options":["Variable substitution","Command substitution","Expanding * into a list of files","Mandatory removal of all spaces"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Double quotes preserve $variable and $(command) substitution but block pathname expansion.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you print a literal dollar sign while still substituting the value of HOME?","options":["echo \"Cost: \\$5; home: $HOME\"","echo 'Cost: $5; home: $HOME'","echo Cost: $5; home: $HOME","echo $(HOME)"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Only the dollar sign in the price is escaped; $HOME is still substituted.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does the $(date) construct do?","options":["Substitutes the output of date","Always prints the literal word date","Creates a directory named date","Assigns a variable named date"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Command substitution replaces the construct with command output.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does !! do in interactive Bash history expansion?","options":["Runs the previous command","Deletes the history","Lists only aliases","Ends the current shell"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"!! repeats the most recent command in history.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you execute history entry number 17?","options":["!17","history=17","$17","!!17 as an exact equivalent"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"!n refers to an absolute history entry number.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does false; echo Ready print?","options":["Ready","Nothing","false Ready","Only a syntax error message"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A semicolon runs the next command regardless of the previous command’s status.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does false && echo Ready print?","options":["Nothing","Ready","false","&&"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"After false fails, the right-hand side of && is skipped.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does true || echo Retry print?","options":["Nothing","Retry","true Retry","A message saying true is unknown"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"true succeeds, so the right-hand side of || is not executed.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you run echo Failed only if check fails?","options":["check || echo Failed","check && echo Failed","check; echo Failed","echo Failed; check"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"|| runs the command on the right if the command on the left fails.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command from the lab shows the current username?","options":["whoami","uname -n","pwd","cal"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"whoami shows the user, uname -n shows the hostname, and pwd shows the directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After an unclosed quote, the shell displays a continuation prompt. What does this mean?","options":["It is waiting for the command to be completed","The whole computer must have frozen","The command has already completed successfully","A file has been deleted automatically"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Quotes must be paired; the shell waits for more input until the command is complete.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 5_ Command Line Skills.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/13Lceo1Wd0Z_mNZMmwj6Hcmz9U-cT81om/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 5 - Command Line Skills.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qNfdjkWSf84C7dmHjf6Rnis6jW-uWmrv/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":6,"title":"Getting Help and Finding Files","en":"Getting Help","intro":"Knowing how to find the right option matters more than memorizing every command. Learn man, info, brief help, and the differences between locating a program, documentation, and an ordinary file.","sections":[{"title":"Reading man pages","body":"man command opens a manual page through a pager, usually less. q exits; Space/PageDown moves forward; b/PageUp moves backward; arrow keys move by line. /term searches forward; n repeats the search in the same direction, while N searches in the opposite direction. h displays pager help."},{"title":"Page structure","body":"NAME gives a brief purpose; SYNOPSIS gives syntax; DESCRIPTION/OPTIONS explain behavior and parameters; FILES lists related files; AUTHOR, REPORTING BUGS, and COPYRIGHT provide authorship and maintenance information; SEE ALSO lists related material. Not every page contains every subsection."},{"title":"SYNOPSIS notation","body":"[item] means an optional element; ... means repetition is allowed; A|B indicates alternatives. In cal [[month] year], a year may be provided alone, but a month requires a year. Documentation brackets should not normally be typed literally in the command."},{"title":"Manual sections","body":"The numbered man categories are: 1, user commands; 2, system calls; 3, library calls; 4, special files; 5, file formats; 6, games; 7, miscellaneous; 8, administration; 9, kernel functions. man 5 passwd opens the file format description, while passwd(1) describes the password-changing command."},{"title":"Searching documentation","body":"man -f name and whatis name display brief information for a page name. man -k keyword and apropos keyword search names and short descriptions for a keyword. This differs from /term inside an already open page."},{"title":"Locating files","body":"which searches PATH for an executable file. whereis looks for commands, source files, and manual pages in typical locations. locate searches paths in a previously built database; locate -c counts matches, and -b restricts matching to the basename. updatedb refreshes the database with appropriate privileges. A new file may not yet appear in the database."},{"title":"Info and other sources","body":"info organizes material into linked nodes: Tab selects a link, Enter opens it, n/p move between neighboring nodes, u moves up, l goes back, and q exits. info without arguments shows the top level. H opens the command list; h opens the tutorial. command --help provides brief help; README files and additional documentation are usually in /usr/share/doc."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"man ls","description":"Open the ls manual."},{"cmd":"man 5 passwd","description":"Open the passwd file format description."},{"cmd":"man -k password","description":"Find pages by keyword."},{"cmd":"whatis passwd","description":"Show short descriptions of pages with this name."},{"cmd":"whereis passwd","description":"Locate the program and its related documentation."},{"cmd":"locate -c passwd","description":"Count matching paths in the locate database."},{"cmd":"locate -b '\\passwd'","description":"Search for the exact basename passwd in the locate implementation used in the course."},{"cmd":"info date","description":"Open the Info documentation for date."},{"cmd":"date --help","description":"Show the brief built-in help for date."}],"lab":[{"task":"Open man date, search for file, move to the next match, and exit.","answer":"man date\nInside the page: /file, Enter, n, q","explanation":"The search runs in the pager; after a forward search, n moves to the next match."},{"task":"Find manual pages related to passwords without knowing the exact command name.","answer":"man -k password\nor apropos password","explanation":"The search covers page names and short descriptions."},{"task":"Open documentation on the passwd file structure rather than the password-changing command.","answer":"man 5 passwd","explanation":"Section 5 covers file formats."},{"task":"Compare searching for a program with searching all paths known to the database.","answer":"whereis passwd\nlocate passwd","explanation":"whereis focuses on programs and documentation; locate searches its path database."},{"task":"Open info date, follow a link, and go back.","answer":"info date\nTab → Enter → l → q","explanation":"Info nodes are connected by hyperlinks; l returns to the previous location."},{"task":"Find the directory containing additional README files for installed packages.","answer":"ls /usr/share/doc","explanation":"This documentation supplements man/info and often includes configuration details."}],"pitfalls":["The NAME subsection within a page and the category number in man(1) or man(5) are different kinds of “section.”","locate does not rescan the entire file system on every invocation.","whereis is not a universal search tool for every user file.","The n key has different meanings in man and info.","Syntax brackets in a SYNOPSIS are usually not entered literally."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you open the manual for cp?","options":["man cp","cp man","manual=cp","info --delete cp"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"man takes the name of the desired page.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which man subsection shows the general form of a command invocation?","options":["SYNOPSIS","COPYRIGHT","AUTHOR","REPORTING BUGS"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"SYNOPSIS briefly describes allowed options and arguments.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What do square brackets mean in a SYNOPSIS?","options":["The element is optional","The brackets must always be typed literally","The element must be repeated twice","The command is available only to root"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"This is documentation notation, not a set of required command characters.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In cal [[month] year], which usage does the syntax allow?","options":["Providing only a year","Providing a month without a year as a documented separate option","Always typing two pairs of square brackets","Always omitting arguments"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The outer group allows a year; the inner group makes the month optional when a year is present.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does ... after [OPTION] mean?","options":["Several elements of the indicated type may be used","The command is in the parent directory","Three dots must be printed","Root is always required"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An ellipsis indicates that the preceding construct may be repeated.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which man category normally describes the format of /etc/passwd?","options":["5","1","6","8"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Category 5 covers file formats and conventions.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need the passwd file description, but man passwd shows the password-changing command. What should you run?","options":["man 5 passwd","passwd 5","man -k 5","man passwd /etc"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A category number before the name selects a specific page.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Enter the man category number for system administration commands.","answer":["8"],"explanation":"Section 8 contains administration commands.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 commands that search man page names and short descriptions by keyword.","options":["apropos copy","man -k copy","man 5 copy","which copy"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"apropos and man -k serve the same purpose.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command corresponds to man -f passwd?","options":["whatis passwd","apropos passwd as a search across all descriptions","locate passwd","pwd passwd"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"whatis displays brief information for a page name.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You searched for /size inside man. Which key moves to the next match forward?","options":["n","q","u","Tab"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"After a forward search, n continues in the same direction.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you leave a man page in the usual less pager?","options":["q","x as the only method","export","Ctrl+L as an exit command"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"q closes the viewer and returns to the shell.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Where should you look for references to related commands in a man page?","options":["SEE ALSO","Only AUTHOR","Only COPYRIGHT","Only the filename in the prompt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"SEE ALSO lists related manuals and other material.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need to find an executable and its man pages in typical locations. Which tool should you choose?","options":["whereis","echo","history","touch"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"whereis focuses on commands, source files, and documentation files.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A new file was created a minute ago, but locate does not show it. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The locate database has not yet been updated","The file definitely does not exist","locate always searches only directories","The file must be renamed with a .gz extension"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"locate uses a snapshot of paths rather than a live disk scan.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tool updates the locate database?","options":["updatedb","update-shell","man -f","whereis -new"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"updatedb rebuilds the search database; updating the system database requires appropriate privileges.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does locate -b restrict the search to?","options":["The basename, excluding the path’s directories","Only binary programs","Only files one byte in size","Only bzip2 archives"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The basename is the final component of a path.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does locate -c pattern output?","options":["The number of matches","The contents of every file","The current directory","Complete compression documentation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The -c option counts matching paths.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which documentation tool organizes topics into linked nodes?","options":["info","whoami","gzip","cp"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Info links documentation nodes into a hierarchy.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You followed a link to another topic in Info. How do you return to the previous view?","options":["l","n as an always-backward command","rm","export"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"l returns to the last visited node or location; u moves up the hierarchy.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command quickly shows brief date usage without opening a pager?","options":["date --help","date --man-only","help-file date as a universal command","man -delete date"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Many commands support --help.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which directory usually contains additional README files for installed packages?","options":["/usr/share/doc","/dev/null","/proc/cpuinfo","/boot/grub as a universal README store"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Packages place additional documentation in /usr/share/doc.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the difference between /password inside man and man -k password?","options":["The first searches the open page; the second searches page names and descriptions","Both always search files on disk","The first deletes matches","The second changes a password"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"These search different levels of the documentation.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 6_ Getting Help.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TAIqJ8GW6T0q91sy0TLSoLWBnuxa6-KJ/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 6 - Getting Help.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/11xWT7ZXQ1OWpzkW6suTrZRlE9FcO2e1N/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":7,"title":"Navigating the File System","en":"Navigating the Filesystem","intro":"Understand the directory tree and distinguish absolute paths, relative paths, home directories, and working directories. Learn to read ls output and choose the right sorting options.","sections":[{"title":"The file system tree","body":"The Linux root directory is /. Devices are mounted into a shared directory hierarchy rather than requiring drive letters such as C: and D:. A directory is a special file system object that associates names with files. The phrase 'everything is a file' emphasizes a consistent approach to accessing data and devices."},{"title":"Home and working directories","body":"A regular user's home directory often looks like /home/username, but this is not mandatory. ~ represents the current user's home; ~bob represents bob's home if that user exists. Root's home is usually /root. HOME stores the home path, while pwd displays the working directory. These values can differ. Permissions determine access to another user's home."},{"title":"Changing directories and using paths","body":"cd path changes the working directory; cd without arguments returns home. An absolute path starts with / and does not depend on the working directory. A relative path starts from the current location. . means the current directory and .. means its parent; for example, ../../Downloads goes up two levels and enters Downloads. Tilde expansion usually produces an absolute path."},{"title":"Listings and hidden files","body":"Without arguments, ls lists visible names in the working directory. ls /path lists the specified directory's contents. A name beginning with a dot is hidden by convention; ls -a includes such names. Colors depend on settings and --color and are not a required feature of the output."},{"title":"Long listings","body":"ls -l shows the type, permissions, hard link count, owner, group, size, modification time, and name. Initial characters include: - regular file, d directory, l symbolic link, s socket, p FIFO, b block device, and c character device. An arrow beside a link points to its target. A directory's size in this listing is not the sum of its contents' sizes."},{"title":"Sizes and directories","body":"ls -lh adds readable K/M/G units to a long listing. ls -ld dir displays information about the directory itself instead of listing its contents. ls -R dir recursively lists subdirectories. Use ls -d pattern to display the matching names themselves."},{"title":"Order and timestamps","body":"By default, ls sorts by name according to the locale. -S sorts by size, largest first; -t sorts by modification time, newest first; -r reverses the chosen order. -R means recursion, not reverse sorting. --full-time displays detailed timestamps. A directory's modification time reflects changes to its entries, not every edit to a file inside it."},{"title":"Patterns from the lab","body":"In ls -d /etc/s*, the asterisk selects names beginning with s. /etc/???? matches names exactly four characters long. /etc/[abcd]* matches names starting with one of the listed characters. The shell expands patterns before starting ls."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"pwd","description":"Display the current working directory."},{"cmd":"cd","description":"Go to the home directory."},{"cmd":"cd ../dict","description":"Go to the sibling directory dict through the parent directory."},{"cmd":"ls -a","description":"Include hidden names in the listing."},{"cmd":"ls -ld /etc","description":"Display metadata for /etc itself."},{"cmd":"ls -lhS","description":"Display a long listing with readable sizes, largest first."},{"cmd":"ls -lrt","description":"Display a long listing ordered from oldest to newest modification."},{"cmd":"ls -R Documents","description":"List Documents and its subdirectory contents."},{"cmd":"ls -d /etc/????","description":"Select four-character names directly inside /etc."}],"lab":[{"task":"From /usr/share/doc, enter its bash subdirectory using a relative path and check the result.","answer":"cd bash\npwd","explanation":"The result is /usr/share/doc/bash if that directory exists."},{"task":"From /usr/share/doc, go to the sibling directory /usr/share/dict.","answer":"cd ../dict\npwd","explanation":".. means /usr/share here, then dict is selected."},{"task":"Display your home and working directories, then return home.","answer":"echo \"$HOME\"\npwd\ncd","explanation":"An ordinary cd does not change HOME; pwd displays the current location."},{"task":"View information about /etc itself, then list its contents separately.","answer":"ls -ld /etc\nls -l /etc","explanation":"The -d option makes ls display the directory object instead of its contents."},{"task":"List hidden files and directories in your home directory.","answer":"ls -a ~","explanation":"A leading dot hides a name from a normal ls listing."},{"task":"Display paths directly inside /etc whose names contain exactly four characters.","answer":"ls -d /etc/????","explanation":"Each ? matches one character, and -d prevents ls from listing the contents of matching directories."}],"pitfalls":["/ is the tree root; /root is usually the administrator's home.","pwd and $HOME answer different questions.","ls -r reverses sorting; ls -R enables recursion.","ls -l dir lists the contents; ls -ld dir describes the directory itself.","A hidden name does not mean the file is protected from access."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Which path represents the root of the Linux file system?","options":["/","/root","~","C:"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The tree root is /; /root is a separate directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A user's HOME is /home/lea and the working directory is /etc. What does pwd display?","options":["/etc","/home/lea","~lea","/root"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"pwd displays the current location, not the home directory.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Where does cd without arguments normally take you?","options":["To the current user's home directory","Always to /","Up one level","To the first directory in PATH"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Without an argument, cd uses the home directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Select 2 absolute paths.","options":["/usr/share/doc","/home/lea/notes.txt","../notes.txt","Documents/notes.txt"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Absolute paths begin at the root, /.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The working directory is /home/lea/Documents/School. Where does cd ../../Downloads take you if the directory exists?","options":["/home/lea/Downloads","/home/Downloads","/home/lea/Documents/Downloads","/Downloads"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The two .. components move from School to Documents, then to /home/lea.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"From /usr/share/doc, which relative path reaches /usr/share/man?","options":["../man","./man","../../man","/man"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"First move up to /usr/share, then enter man.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does a single dot (.) mean in a path?","options":["The current directory","Always the home directory","Always a hidden root","Any letter in a name"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A dot refers to the current directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does ~bob mean if the bob account exists?","options":["Bob's home directory","A file named bob in the current directory","Necessarily /root/bob","The parent of the current directory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A tilde followed by a username expands to that user's home directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The file .profile is absent from a normal ls listing. Which option shows it?","options":["-a","-r","-S","-t"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"ls -a includes names beginning with a dot.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does the initial d in drwxr-xr-x mean?","options":["Directory","The file is deleted","The size is ten","The file is compressed"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The first character in a long listing describes the object type.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A line starts with lrwxrwxrwx and contains an -> arrow. What is this object?","options":["A symbolic link","A regular directory","A ZIP archive","A socket"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"l indicates a symbolic link, and the arrow shows its target.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In '-rw-r--r-- 1 lea staff 420 Nov 1 10:00 notes', what does 420 mean?","options":["The file size in bytes","The owner's UID","The number of lines","The hard link count"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The size follows the owner and group; the link count appears earlier.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you view the permissions and owner of the Documents directory itself?","options":["ls -ld Documents","ls -l Documents","ls -R Documents","cd Documents"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With -d, ls displays the directory's metadata instead of its child entries.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How should you interpret a directory size shown by ls -l?","options":["It is not the total size of all files inside it","It is always the size of the entire tree","It is always the number of users","It is the amount of free memory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The size of a directory object refers to its own internal structure.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need to list nested subdirectories and their contents. Which ls option is required?","options":["-R","-r","-d","-h"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-R enables recursive traversal.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What order does ls -lS use?","options":["Largest files first","Smallest files first","Only by time, oldest first","Always random"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Uppercase -S sorts by size in descending order.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you list entries from oldest to newest modification?","options":["ls -lrt","ls -lt","ls -lS","ls -la"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-t selects time sorting; -r reverses the normal newest-first order.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which ls option displays the full timestamp?","options":["--full-time","--all-sizes","--complete-path","--clock-only"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"--full-time displays detailed timestamps and enables the long format.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The directory /etc/abcd contains files. Why does ls -d /etc/???? show /etc/abcd rather than its contents?","options":["-d requests the matching directory itself","? automatically creates an archive","Four ? characters mean search only for regular files","ls cannot read directories"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"? selects the name length, while -d controls how ls handles directories.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Select 2 true statements about a directory's modification time.","options":["Adding a file entry changes its contents","Removing a file entry changes its contents","Every read of a contained file necessarily changes the directory's mtime","A directory's mtime always equals the Linux boot time"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A directory contains name entries; adding or removing entries changes that structure.","difficulty":"basic"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 7 - Navigating the Filesystem.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oerWiIO-FhG7TqBeu1XHAfY1xZJ39HUu/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 7_ Navigating the Filesystem.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kiRyMow2LxnqWz0eXjGGxJZFs4FitY88/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":8,"title":"Managing Files","en":"Managing Files and Directories","intro":"Master filename patterns and basic operations: create, copy, rename, move, and delete. Always distinguish the source file, target, and destination directory.","sections":[{"title":"Names and globbing","body":"Linux is case-sensitive: notes.txt and Notes.txt are different names. The shell expands glob patterns before running a command; they select filenames, not lines inside files. With normal Bash settings, * does not include names beginning with a dot."},{"title":"Asterisk and question mark","body":"* matches zero or more characters; ? matches exactly one. a* selects names starting with a, *.txt selects names ending in .txt, and a?? selects three-character names starting with a. ?????*s requires at least six characters and a final s. Quoting can prevent pattern expansion."},{"title":"Sets and negation","body":"[abc] matches one listed character, and [0-9] matches one digit. [!DP]* selects names whose first character is neither D nor P. A bracket expression always matches one character; ranges depend on matching rules and locale, so use simple ranges for educational ASCII examples."},{"title":"Inspecting matches","body":"echo pattern displays expanded names. If a glob passes a directory to ls, ordinary ls displays that directory's contents. ls -d pattern displays the matching objects themselves. Before a bulk operation, check which names have been selected."},{"title":"Copying","body":"cp source destination creates a copy. If the target is an existing directory, the original filename is retained; if it is a new filename, the copy receives that name. -v reports actions; -i asks before overwriting; -n does not overwrite an existing target. -p preserves supported attributes within the user's permissions. Copying a directory requires -r or -R."},{"title":"Moving and renaming","body":"mv source destination moves an object and also renames it when given a new name. After a successful move, the original name is gone. mv can move directories without -r. Its -i, -n, and -v options have meanings similar to those of cp."},{"title":"Creating and deleting","body":"touch newfile creates an empty file if it does not exist; it does not empty an existing file. mkdir creates a directory. rm deletes files; -i asks for confirmation, and -r removes a directory tree. rmdir removes only empty directories. rm does not move objects into a typical desktop trash folder."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"echo [DP]*","description":"View names starting with D or P."},{"cmd":"ls -d *[0-9]*","description":"Display names containing a digit without listing the contents of matching directories."},{"cmd":"cp -iv notes.txt backup.txt","description":"Copy while reporting the action and asking before overwriting."},{"cmd":"cp -p /etc/hosts hosts.copy","description":"Copy while preserving permitted attributes."},{"cmd":"cp -R project project-copy","description":"Copy a directory tree."},{"cmd":"mv old.txt new.txt","description":"Rename a file."},{"cmd":"touch draft.txt","description":"Create an empty file if it does not exist."},{"cmd":"mkdir practice","description":"Create the practice directory."},{"cmd":"rmdir empty-dir","description":"Remove an empty directory."}],"lab":[{"task":"A practice directory contains draft.txt, data.csv, notes.txt, and a directory named docs. Select only names ending in .txt.","answer":"ls -d *.txt","explanation":"draft.txt and notes.txt match; the pattern checks names."},{"task":"Copy /etc/hosts to the current practice directory as hosts.copy and display the action.","answer":"cp -v /etc/hosts hosts.copy","explanation":"The source remains, and a separate copy is created with a new name."},{"task":"Copy hosts.copy to hosts.backup without overwriting an existing backup.","answer":"cp -n hosts.copy hosts.backup","explanation":"No clobber leaves an existing target unchanged without an interactive prompt."},{"task":"Create Myetc, copy the /etc/udev tree into it, and inspect the result.","answer":"mkdir Myetc\ncp -R /etc/udev Myetc\nls -lR Myetc","explanation":"If access is permitted, a udev subdirectory and its contents appear inside the existing Myetc directory."},{"task":"Create an empty file named premove and rename it to postmove.","answer":"touch premove\nmv premove postmove\nls -l postmove","explanation":"mv changes the name; no premove copy remains."},{"task":"Create and remove an empty scratch directory inside the practice directory.","answer":"mkdir scratch\nrmdir scratch","explanation":"rmdir works only for an empty directory; it fails if the directory is not empty."},{"task":"Copy the source file while preserving available timestamps and permissions, then compare the listings.","answer":"cp -p /etc/hosts hosts.saved\nls -l /etc/hosts hosts.saved","explanation":"An ordinary user may be unable to retain root ownership even when timestamps and mode are preserved."}],"pitfalls":["* and ? differ: * can match an empty string.","[abc] matches one character, not the string abc.","cp -r is recursive, but ls -r reverses sorting.","Moving directories with mv does not require -r.","Using touch on an existing file does not empty its contents.","cp and mv can overwrite the target; rm normally bypasses the trash."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"What expands *.txt before a command runs?","options":["The shell","Only cp","The kernel acting as a separate text editor","Only a graphical file manager"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Globbing is a shell feature, so it works with many commands.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 names match a*?","options":["a","alpha","beta","Alpha"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The asterisk can match zero characters, but the lowercase a remains significant.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which name matches log?.txt?","options":["log7.txt","log.txt","log12.txt","xlog7.txt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"? matches exactly one character.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 names match [DP]*?","options":["Data","Public","Music","alphaDP"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The bracket expression applies only to the first character.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does [!ab]* select among ordinary non-hidden names?","options":["Names whose first character is neither a nor b","Names with no a or b anywhere","Only the name !ab","All names starting with ab"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The negation applies to one character within the bracket expression.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which pattern selects names containing at least one digit?","options":["*[0-9]*","[0-9] without asterisks matches all such names","[!0-9]*","*.digit"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The asterisks allow any prefix and suffix around one required digit.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"What is the minimum length of a name matching ?????*s? Enter a number.","answer":["6"],"explanation":"Five ? characters require five characters, followed by a mandatory s; * can be empty.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why can ls -d a* be more useful than ls a* for checking selected objects?","options":["It displays matching directory names rather than their contents","It automatically deletes directories","It disables all matches","It selects only files created today"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Without -d, ls lists child names when given a directory argument.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"backup is an existing directory. What does cp report.txt backup do?","options":["Creates or overwrites backup/report.txt","Renames report.txt to a directory","Deletes report.txt after copying","Always creates a file literally named backup instead of using the directory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An existing destination directory retains the source's base filename.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you request confirmation before overwriting a copy destination?","options":["cp -i a.txt b.txt","cp -v a.txt b.txt","cp -r a.txt b.txt","cp -p a.txt b.txt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-i means interactive; -v only reports actions.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need a copy, but an existing destination file must remain unchanged without a prompt. Which option is taught for this?","options":["-n","-i","-v","-r"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-n means no clobber and prevents overwriting.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you copy a directory and its contained files?","options":["cp -R project backup","cp project backup with no options","cp -l-only project backup","mv project backup as a copy operation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A recursive directory copy requires -R or -r.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the purpose of cp -p?","options":["Preserve the source attributes that permissions allow","Always change the owner to root","Display help only","Delete the original"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-p preserves mode, timestamps, and ownership within the available permissions.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does mv old.txt new.txt do if new.txt does not exist?","options":["Renames old.txt to new.txt","Creates a second copy and retains the old name","Merges the text of two files","Creates a directory named old.txt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"mv moves the name, so the original path disappears after success.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Moving the project directory into an existing archive directory requires...","options":["mv project archive","mv -r project archive as the mandatory syntax","cp project archive without recursion","touch project archive"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"mv can move directories without a recursive option.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the size of a previously nonexistent file immediately after touch empty.txt?","options":["0 bytes","Always 1 KB","The size of the working directory","It necessarily contains the username"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"touch creates an empty file if it is absent.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you create the practice directory?","options":["mkdir practice","touch practice to create a directory","pwd practice","cd --create practice"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"mkdir creates a directory; touch creates a regular file.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The old directory contains a file. What does rmdir old do?","options":["Refuses to remove the nonempty directory","Deletes the entire tree without a prompt","Creates old.tar","Moves the file into the home directory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"rmdir is intended only for empty directories.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which rm option asks for confirmation before deletion?","options":["-i","-v","-p","-S"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The interactive option lets you confirm an individual deletion.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"cp a.txt b.txt is followed by mv b.txt c.txt. Both succeed, and initially only a.txt existed. Which 2 names remain?","options":["a.txt","c.txt","b.txt","a.txt.backup"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"cp leaves the original in place; mv then replaces the name b.txt with c.txt.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 8_ Managing Files and Directories.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Fw4GgQDdlOKNEqBbv02WSGRTopw_kW4Q/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 8 - Managing Files and Directories.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-SuLjFDuDU3HQJK9UnElLwAMgMFua31E/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":9,"title":"Archives and Compression","en":"Archiving and Compression","intro":"Archiving collects files in one container; compression reduces their data representation. Practice creating, listing, and extracting tar and ZIP archives separately.","sections":[{"title":"Why archive files?","body":"Archives are useful for transferring multiple files, making backups, and storing old logs. Archiving and compression can be used separately or together. An uncompressed tar archive is often slightly larger than the combined source data because of metadata and padding."},{"title":"Lossless and lossy compression","body":"Lossless compression restores the original bytes, which programs, documents, and logs require. Lossy compression discards some information and suits certain images and audio. In the sense discussed here, PNG/GIF use lossless compression and JPEG uses lossy compression. Already compressed data often compresses poorly a second time; effectiveness depends on the content."},{"title":"gzip, bzip2, and xz","body":"gzip file normally replaces the source with file.gz; gunzip or gzip -d restores it. bzip2/bunzip2 use .bz2, and xz/unxz use .xz. gzip -l reports sizes and compression savings. Algorithms make different tradeoffs between speed, memory, and size; bzip2 or xz is not guaranteed to produce the best result for every file."},{"title":"tar modes","body":"tar -c creates an archive, -t lists its contents, and -x extracts it. -f specifies the archive filename and requires an argument; -v reports processed names. tar -cf backup.tar dir automatically includes nested directories. -r appends files to an existing uncompressed tar archive."},{"title":"tar with compression","body":"-z uses gzip; -j uses bzip2. Examples: tar -czf backup.tar.gz dir and tar -cjf backup.tar.bz2 dir. Extensions such as .tar.gz/.tgz and .tar.bz2/.tbz/.tbz2 are conventions; renaming alone does not change a format. Place f last in a group of short options and follow it immediately with the archive name."},{"title":"Listing and extraction","body":"tar -tf archive.tar lists names without extracting. tar -xzf archive.tar.gz extracts into the working directory, preserving relative paths. To extract one file, specify its exact name from the archive listing. When archiving absolute paths, ordinary tar removes the leading /; in the lab, etc/udev is restored inside the working directory. Extraction leaves the archive itself in place."},{"title":"ZIP","body":"zip archive.zip files creates or updates a ZIP archive and normally leaves source files in place. A directory tree requires zip -r archive.zip dir. unzip -l lists the contents; unzip archive.zip extracts them. To extract one object, specify its path inside the ZIP. When names conflict, unzip may ask about replacement; an empty practice directory is useful."},{"title":"Practical comparison","body":"Compare sizes using the same data and restore the original file after each test. In the lab example, xz produced a smaller file than gzip, while bzip2 produced a larger one; this is a result for that file, not a universal ranking. tar -r means append; zip -r means recursion."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"gzip notes.txt","description":"Compress one file, normally replacing it with notes.txt.gz."},{"cmd":"gunzip notes.txt.gz","description":"Restore the uncompressed file."},{"cmd":"bzip2 notes.txt","description":"Compress to notes.txt.bz2."},{"cmd":"bunzip2 notes.txt.bz2","description":"Decompress bzip2 data."},{"cmd":"xz notes.txt","description":"Compress to notes.txt.xz."},{"cmd":"unxz notes.txt.xz","description":"Decompress xz data."},{"cmd":"tar -czvf backup.tar.gz project","description":"Create a gzip-compressed tar archive and report filenames."},{"cmd":"tar -tf backup.tar","description":"List an archive without extracting it."},{"cmd":"tar -xjf backup.tar.bz2","description":"Extract a bzip2-compressed tar archive."},{"cmd":"tar -rf backup.tar notes.txt","description":"Append a file to an uncompressed tar archive."},{"cmd":"zip -r project.zip project","description":"Create a ZIP containing the entire project tree."},{"cmd":"unzip -l project.zip","description":"List ZIP contents without extraction."}],"lab":[{"task":"Create an uncompressed practice archive of /etc/udev and list it.","answer":"mkdir mybackups\ntar -cvf mybackups/udev.tar /etc/udev\ntar -tvf mybackups/udev.tar","explanation":"tar adds the tree recursively; the leading / is normally removed from names."},{"task":"Create a gzip-compressed archive of /etc/udev.","answer":"tar -czvf mybackups/udev.tar.gz /etc/udev\nls -lh mybackups","explanation":"The difference from an ordinary tar archive is gzip compression enabled by -z."},{"task":"Extract udev.tar.gz in a separate directory named mybackups.","answer":"cd mybackups\ntar -xzvf udev.tar.gz\nls etc/udev","explanation":"The etc/udev entry is created relative to the working directory; the original archive remains."},{"task":"Append /etc/hosts to the existing uncompressed udev.tar and check the result.","answer":"tar -rvf udev.tar /etc/hosts\ntar -tvf udev.tar","explanation":"-r means append; the archive entry will normally be named etc/hosts."},{"task":"Compress and restore a practice notes.txt file with each of the three tools.","answer":"gzip notes.txt\ngunzip notes.txt.gz\nbzip2 notes.txt\nbunzip2 notes.txt.bz2\nxz notes.txt\nunxz notes.txt.xz","explanation":"Compression normally replaces the original filename; decompression restores it."},{"task":"Create a ZIP of the project tree and check its listing without extracting data.","answer":"zip -r project.zip project\nunzip -l project.zip","explanation":"ZIP requires -r to include directory contents; unzip -l only lists them."}],"pitfalls":["tar by itself does not have to compress data.","A filename extension does not convert the file format.","tar's -f requires an archive name; do not accidentally give it another option letter.","gzip replaces the source file by default; zip normally retains it.","tar includes nested directories automatically; zip requires -r.","Names used for selective extraction must match paths inside the archive."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"How does archiving differ from compression?","options":["Archiving groups files; compression reduces their data representation","They are always the same operation","Compression necessarily creates a directory tree","Archiving always destroys source files"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"You can have an uncompressed archive or an individually compressed file.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which type of compression is required for an executable that must be restored byte for byte?","options":["Lossless","Lossy","Only JPEG","Any type; the data does not matter"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Losing even some bytes can corrupt a program.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does recompressing an already compressed image often provide little benefit?","options":["Most redundancy has already been removed","Images contain no bytes","gzip always refuses to read images","PNG is necessarily uncompressed"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A new algorithm may barely reduce the size of already compressed data.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What normally remains after a successful gzip report.txt with no extra options?","options":["report.txt.gz instead of report.txt","Always both files","Only a directory named report.txt","A file named report.zip"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Normal gzip compression replaces the source with a compressed file.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Select 2 ways to restore report.txt.gz.","options":["gunzip report.txt.gz","gzip -d report.txt.gz","zip report.txt.gz","tar -c report.txt.gz"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"gunzip and gzip's -d mode perform decompression.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which extension does xz normally create?","options":[".xz",".bz2",".gz",".tar without any additional operation"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The xz compressor uses the .xz extension.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you decompress data.bz2 using bzip2's dedicated companion command?","options":["bunzip2 data.bz2","unxz data.bz2","gzip data.bz2 as the correct decompression command","zip -r data.bz2"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"bunzip2 restores bzip2-compressed data.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does gzip -l data.gz do?","options":["Displays information about the compressed and original sizes","Extracts all directories","Deletes the archive","Creates a new symbolic link"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The -l mode displays statistics rather than decompressing the file.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command creates an uncompressed tar archive of project?","options":["tar -cf project.tar project","tar -xf project.tar project","tar -tf project.tar project","gzip project.tar project as an equivalent command"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-c selects creation; -f supplies the archive filename.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tar option enables gzip?","options":["-z","-j","-t","-r"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-z selects gzip; -j selects bzip2.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tar option lists archive members without extraction?","options":["-t","-x","-c","-r"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"List mode (-t) displays names without restoring files.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command creates a bzip2-compressed tar archive?","options":["tar -cjf project.tar.bz2 project","tar -czf project.tar.bz2 project as bzip2 compression","tar -xjf project.tar.bz2 project","zip -j project.tar.bz2 project"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-j selects the format; an extension alone does not change the algorithm.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the problem with tar -xjfv data.tbz in the syntax discussed here?","options":["-f takes v as the archive filename","It always deletes data.tbz","-j becomes Java","It simply enables all flags regardless of order"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The -f option consumes its argument; use tar -xjvf data.tbz to avoid ambiguity.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An archive entry is named School/Art/linux.txt. How do you extract only that entry?","options":["tar -xjf school.tbz School/Art/linux.txt","tar -xjf school.tbz linux.txt without the path as the exact name","tar -cjf school.tbz School/Art/linux.txt","tar -tjf school.tbz as an extraction command"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The path must match the archive entry, and -x selects extraction.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An archive with the relative entry etc/hosts is extracted in /home/lea/restore. Where does the file appear?","options":["/home/lea/restore/etc/hosts","Always /etc/hosts","Always /root/hosts","Only inside the original archive; no extraction occurs"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"By default, relative paths are restored from the working directory.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you append notes.txt to an existing uncompressed backup.tar?","options":["tar -rf backup.tar notes.txt","tar -cf backup.tar notes.txt as an append without recreation","tar -tf backup.tar notes.txt","gzip -r backup.tar notes.txt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-r means append; -c creates a new archive.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is required to include all files in subdirectories when creating a ZIP?","options":["zip -r backup.zip project","zip backup.zip project without -r for the entire tree","unzip -l project","zip -f as the required recursion option"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Unlike tar, ZIP does not automatically include an entire directory tree's contents.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you list ZIP contents without extracting them?","options":["unzip -l backup.zip","zip -x backup.zip","gzip -l backup.zip as a universal ZIP listing command","tar -c backup.zip"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"unzip's -l option lists files.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Select 2 correct statements about source files.","options":["zip normally leaves source files in place","Extracting a tar archive normally retains the archive itself","gzip without special options always retains the original","unzip must delete the ZIP after extraction"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Distinguish a compressor replacing a file from archive creation or extraction.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In the lab, the same file produced a 260 KB .gz, a 346 KB .bz2, and a 199 KB .xz. Which conclusion is justified?","options":["xz produced the smallest file in this test; results depend on the data","xz will always be best for every dataset","bzip2 can never compress data","gzip uses lossy compression"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"This comparison describes one dataset, not universal superiority.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 9 - Archiving and Compression.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1NbcfUtceUxzJTcRLmAUVFnVFdD4EQqty/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 9_ Archiving and Compression.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gkDZyqDqP2H_v6nPVfKFbe87OGL3FEn9/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":10,"title":"Working with Text","en":"Working With Text","intro":"Streams let you connect simple commands: obtain data, select lines, extract fields, and save the result. For the exam, trace the processing order and distinguish shell patterns from grep regular expressions.","sections":[{"title":"Viewing files and navigating","body":"cat displays contents and concatenates files. more and less display text a page at a time; Space moves one page, Enter one line, and q quits. less also supports moving backward, /pattern for forward searches, ?pattern for backward searches, n to repeat a search, and N to reverse its direction. head and tail display the first or last 10 lines by default. head -n -3 excludes the last 3 lines; tail -n +3 starts at line 3. tail -f watches for appended data."},{"title":"Streams, pipelines, and redirection","body":"stdin is descriptor 0, stdout is 1, and stderr is 2. | passes a command's stdout to the next command's stdin; stderr is not automatically included. > creates or truncates a file, >> appends, 2> redirects errors, and < reads input from a file. command > all.txt 2>&1 combines the streams; Bash also supports &>. The order of redirections involving 2>&1 matters. Command order matters too: numbering lines before selecting the last ones differs from selecting lines before numbering them."},{"title":"Sorting, statistics, and columns","body":"sort sorts lines; -n compares numbers, -r reverses the order, -t specifies a delimiter, and -k selects a key. Use -k3,3 to select only the third field. wc reports line, word, and byte counts; -l, -w, and -c select a count. A byte is not always a character, especially in UTF-8. cut -d: -f1,5-7 extracts fields; cut -c1-4 extracts character positions. tr transforms characters and reads data from stdin."},{"title":"Filtering with grep","body":"grep prints entire lines containing a match. -i ignores case, -v selects nonmatching lines, -n adds original line numbers, -c counts matching lines, and -w matches whole words. --color highlights matches. grep -F searches for literal text; the lab also uses its older name, fgrep."},{"title":"Basic and extended regular expressions","body":"A dot (.) matches one character, [abc] matches one listed character, [0-9] a digit, and [^0-9] one nondigit. * repeats the preceding element zero or more times: re*d matches rd, red, and reed. ^ and $ anchor the start and end of a line; ^$ matches an empty line. A backslash escapes a special character. With grep -E, + means one or more, ? zero or one, | alternation, (...) grouping, and {3} three repetitions. Single quotes protect the pattern from shell processing."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd | sort | more","description":"Extract usernames, sort them, and view them a page at a time."},{"cmd":"grep -nE 'error|warning' app.log","description":"Search for two alternatives and show line numbers."},{"cmd":"tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < input.txt > upper.txt","description":"Convert Latin letters from a file to uppercase."},{"cmd":"find /etc -name hosts > found.txt 2> errors.txt","description":"Separate normal output from diagnostics."}],"lab":[{"task":"Save Hello in message, then append Again. What should you check?","answer":"echo 'Hello' > message\necho 'Again' >> message\ncat message","explanation":"After both commands, the file contains both lines. Using > instead of >> for the second command would destroy Hello."},{"task":"Obtain a sorted list of usernames from /etc/passwd and view it a page at a time.","answer":"cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd | sort | less","explanation":"Each stage receives the previous stage's stdout; cut selects the first field."},{"task":"Display the first 2 and last 5 lines of a file, then find entries starting with root.","answer":"head -n 2 /etc/passwd\ntail -n 5 /etc/passwd\ngrep '^root:' /etc/passwd","explanation":"The anchor and colon distinguish the exact username root from rootbackup or mentions of /root in other fields."},{"task":"Find lines containing three consecutive digits, then search for the literal text a*b.","answer":"grep -E '[0-9]{3}' data.txt\ngrep -F 'a*b' data.txt","explanation":"The first pattern can match within a longer digit sequence. In the second command, * is not an operator."}],"pitfalls":["grep -c counts lines, not all individual matches.","[^0-9] does not mean 'a line without digits': a7 also contains a nondigit.","In a regex, * repeats the previous element; in a glob, *.txt selects filenames.","Do not use > to redirect a command's output into its own input file: the shell may truncate it before it is read."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"After echo A > note and echo B > note, what does note contain?","options":["A and B","Only B","Only A","An empty file"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"> truncates an existing file before writing.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you keep the existing log and append a new line?","options":["echo done < run.log","echo done > run.log","echo done >> run.log","echo done | run.log"],"answer":[2],"explanation":">> appends stdout to the end of the file.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which file descriptor number represents stderr?","answer":["2"],"explanation":"The standard descriptors are stdin 0, stdout 1, and stderr 2.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"find /etc -name hosts > out.txt still displays Permission denied on the screen. Why?","options":["find forbids redirection","The error is sent to stderr","out.txt is a directory","stdout is always duplicated"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"> redirects only stdout; redirecting errors requires 2>.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you redirect stdout and stderr to one file in the correct order?","options":["cmd > all.txt 2>&1","cmd 2< all.txt","cmd | all.txt","cmd < all.txt 2>1"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"First stdout is redirected to the file; then stderr duplicates stdout's destination.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does an ordinary pipeline cmd1 | cmd2 pass?","options":["cmd2's stdin to cmd1's stderr","cmd1's stdout to cmd2's stdin","Both cmd1 output streams to a file named cmd2","Only the exit status"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"An ordinary | connects standard output to standard input.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file has 30 lines. How many does head -n -7 file display?","options":["7","30","23","0"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A negative -n argument in GNU head excludes the specified number of final lines.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file contains lines 1–12. What does tail -n +9 file display?","options":["The last 9 lines","Lines 1–9","Only line 9","Lines 9–12"],"answer":[3],"explanation":"A plus sign means start at that line, rather than count backward from the end.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you watch lines that an application appends to a log?","options":["head -f app.log","tail -f app.log","cut -f app.log","sort -f app.log"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"tail -f keeps watching for new data.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which key exits less? Enter one character.","answer":["q"],"explanation":"q ends the viewer and returns to the shell prompt.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After searching for /disk in less, which character repeats the search in the opposite direction?","options":["n","N","x","!"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"n repeats in the current search direction; N reverses it.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Lines containing 2, 11, and 3 must be ordered numerically. Which command is appropriate?","options":["sort -n nums","sort -r nums","wc -n nums","cut -n nums"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-n compares numbers; ordinary text sorting may place 11 before 2.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does cut -d: -f1,3 users do?","options":["Prints lines 1–3","Prints fields 1 and 3 using : as the delimiter","Removes : characters","Sorts by the third field"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-d sets the delimiter; -f selects fields.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file contains only the line error error error. What count does grep -c error return?","options":["3","0","1","9"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The -c count refers to matching lines, not occurrences of the word.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need to exclude lines containing nologin from /etc/passwd. Select the command.","options":["grep -i nologin /etc/passwd","grep -v nologin /etc/passwd","grep -n nologin /etc/passwd","grep -w nologin /etc/passwd"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-v inverts the selection, retaining lines without a match.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 lines match ^r..t$?","options":["root","rot","r00t","rooted"],"answer":[0,2],"explanation":"Exactly two characters must appear between r and t, and the anchors constrain the entire line.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which pattern selects only empty lines?","options":[".*","^$","[ ]","$^."],"answer":[1],"explanation":"There are no characters between the start and end of the line.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 lines match ^re*d$?","options":["rd","reed","read","redder"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"e* permits zero or more e characters, but no a or extra characters after d.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does grep 'z*' match a line containing no z?","options":["* means negation","Zero occurrences of z are allowed","grep ignores z","Without -E, the search always succeeds"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A zero-length match is possible in any line.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which expression searches for gray OR grey?","options":["grep 'gray|grey' file","grep -E 'gray|grey' file","grep -v 'gray|grey' file","grep -F 'gray|grey' file"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"In extended regular expressions, | separates alternatives; -F searches for a literal vertical bar.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which line does NOT match [^0-9]?","options":["abc","123","a1","7!"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"123 contains only digits; the other lines contain at least one nondigit.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you obtain the number of lines in a file?","options":["wc -w file","wc -c file","wc -l file","wc -f file"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"-l counts newline characters; for an ordinary line-based file, this is the line count.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' file.txt reports an extra argument. How do you fix it?","options":["tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < file.txt","tr -f file.txt","tr file.txt | 'A-Z'","tr > file.txt"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"tr reads the text to transform from stdin, not from a filename argument.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file has 20 nonempty lines. How does nl file | tail -5 differ from tail -5 file | nl?","options":["There is no difference","The first numbers them 16–20; the second numbers them 1–5","The first numbers them 1–5; the second numbers them 16–20","The first deletes lines from the file"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Numbering before selection preserves the original numbers; numbering after selection starts over.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 10_ Working With Text.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mFFoixNaezLnf_moXb906pOQGga57Uq9/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 10 - Working With Text.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eTDq83DX3e09YE90gn-RRVKkCbK5n8za/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":11,"title":"Scripts and Editors","en":"Basic Scripting","intro":"A shell script is an ordinary text file containing commands. It automates a repeated sequence; variables store data, conditions select actions, and loops repeat them. You do not need long programs for this chapter: focus on reading short snippets confidently.","sections":[{"title":"Execution and the shebang","body":"A #!/bin/bash line at the very beginning selects the interpreter for direct execution. ./task.sh specifies a file in the working directory and requires execute permission. bash task.sh starts the explicitly selected Bash interpreter and requires permission to read the script, but no execute bit on the script itself. If task.sh is not found as a command, check PATH: the current directory is normally absent from it."},{"title":"nano","body":"nano file opens a simple text editor. Ctrl+O saves, Ctrl+X exits, Ctrl+W searches, Ctrl+K cuts a line, Ctrl+U pastes, and Ctrl+G displays help. In the shortcuts at the bottom, ^ means Ctrl. After Ctrl+X with unsaved changes, confirm saving with Y and confirm the filename with Enter."},{"title":"vi: modes, movement, and edits","body":"vi starts in command mode. i inserts before the cursor and a after it; I and A insert at the start and end of the line; o/O open a new line below/above. Esc returns to command mode. h/j/k/l move left/down/up/right; w/e/b move by words; 0/$ go to the start/end of the line; nG goes to line n; G goes to the end of the file. x/X delete a character at/before the cursor, dw deletes a word, dd a line, D the rest of the line, and cw changes a word. A number repeats an action: 2dd. u undoes, yw/yy copy, p/P paste after/before, J joins lines, and ~ toggles case."},{"title":"vi: searching and saving","body":"/text searches forward, ?text backward, and n repeats in the current direction. :%s/old/new/g replaces all matches throughout the file. :w saves; :wq, :x, and ZZ save and exit; :q! exits without saving; :e! reloads the file and discards changes. An exclamation mark does not grant root privileges or bypass file system restrictions."},{"title":"Variables, input, and status","body":"NAME=value assigns a value with no spaces around =; $NAME expands it. Double quotes allow expansion while preserving spaces. read NAME reads input. $1 is the first argument, $0 is the script name, and $? is the last command's exit status: 0 normally means success, and a nonzero code means another result or an error. grep returns 1 when no match is found; -q suppresses normal output. Command substitution $(pwd), like the backticks used in the course, captures command output. exit 1 ends the script with status 1; # starts a comment."},{"title":"Conditions and loops","body":"if command; then ...; else ...; fi tests whether a command succeeds. test and [ ... ] compare values: -eq/-ne for numbers, =/!= for strings, -lt/-le/-gt for numeric order, -f for a regular file, -d for a directory, -x for execute permission, and ! for negation. Spaces are required around [ and ]. elif adds checks; case selects the first matching branch, ;; ends that branch, and esac closes case. for iterates through a list; while repeats its body while the condition is true. do/done delimit the body. $((i + 1)) evaluates arithmetic, and seq produces a numeric sequence."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"chmod u+x task.sh","description":"Allow the owner to execute the script directly."},{"cmd":"bash task.sh","description":"Read the script using the Bash interpreter."},{"cmd":"if [ -d /tmp ]; then echo yes; fi","description":"A short check for a directory's existence."},{"cmd":"for n in 1 2 3; do echo \"$n\"; done","description":"Print three values one at a time."}],"lab":[{"task":"In vi, delete two lines, undo the deletion, and save the file.","answer":"Esc → 2dd → u → :w → Enter","explanation":"2dd is one action affecting two lines, so one u is enough."},{"task":"Create a script that greets its first argument.","answer":"#!/bin/bash\necho \"Hello $1\"","explanation":"After saving, run bash hello.sh Aida; the output is Hello Aida."},{"task":"What should you check if ./sample.sh works but sample.sh does not?","answer":"echo \"$PATH\"","explanation":"An explicit path bypasses the PATH search. To run a script by name, its directory must be in PATH."},{"task":"Write a short loop to create test1, test2, and test3 in a practice directory.","answer":"for n in 1 2 3; do touch \"test$n\"; done","explanation":"for assigns each list item to n; quotes keep the constructed filename as one argument."},{"task":"How many times does the body of while [ \"$num\" -le 100 ] run if the successive inputs are 25, 99, and 101, and the next read is inside the body?","answer":"Twice.","explanation":"25 and 99 satisfy the condition; 101 exits the loop."}],"pitfalls":["NAME = value is not a Bash assignment.","01 and 1 are numerically equal but differ as strings.","The -f test means a regular file; it does not treat a directory as a regular file.","After each new command, $? refers to that command rather than an earlier check."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"What does #!/bin/bash specify when a script is executed directly?","options":["Output directory","Interpreter","Owner's name","Argument list"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The shebang specifies the program that reads the executable text.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A script is readable but has no execute permission. How can you run it with Bash?","options":["./script.sh","bash script.sh","chmod script.sh","exec -r script.sh"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Bash reads the file as data; direct execution with ./ requires execute permission.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does an executable file in the current directory work as ./run.sh but not as run.sh?","options":["Searching for .sh names is forbidden","The current directory is not in PATH",".sh files require root","The shebang ignores dots"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Without a /, the shell searches PATH; ./ specifies a path explicitly.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which line correctly assigns a variable?","options":["TOPIC = Linux","$TOPIC=Linux","TOPIC=Linux","set TOPIC Linux"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"An assignment has no $ before the name and no spaces around =.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After NAME=Sam; echo \"Hi $NAME\", what is printed?","options":["Hi NAME","Hi $NAME","Hi Sam","Sam Hi"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Double quotes allow variable expansion.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"How is a shell script's first argument referenced?","answer":["$1"],"explanation":"$0 is the script name; $1 is the first argument passed to it.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you store a name entered by the user in the NAME variable?","options":["echo NAME","read NAME","read $NAME","input NAME"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"read takes the variable name without a $ sign.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"grep -q absent file exits with status 1. What does this normally mean?","options":["A match was found","No match was found","The file was successfully deleted","Necessarily a permission denial"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"grep distinguishes found (0), not found (1), and errors; -q only suppresses normal output.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which exit status normally indicates success?","answer":["0"],"explanation":"Shell conditions treat status 0 as a true command result.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does HERE=$(pwd) store?","options":["The literal characters pwd","The home directory's contents","The output of pwd: the current directory path","The command's PID"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Command substitution executes pwd and substitutes its standard output.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Select the correct test for whether /tmp is a directory.","options":["[ -d /tmp ]","[-d /tmp]","[ -f /tmp ]","test /tmp -dir"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-d tests for a directory; [ and ] must be separate arguments.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement about the values 01 and 1 is correct?","options":["They differ as both strings and numbers","They are numerically equal but differ as strings","They are equal as strings but differ as numbers","Bash cannot compare these values"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-eq compares numbers; = compares text.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How many lines does for x in red blue green; do echo \"$x\"; done print?","options":["1","2","3","Infinitely many"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The body runs once per list item.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"i=0; while [ \"$i\" -lt 3 ]; do echo \"$i\"; i=$((i+1)); done — what is the last value printed?","options":["0","2","3","4"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The loop prints 0, 1, and 2; at i=3 the condition is false.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which case branch normally serves as the default?","options":["?)","*)","default:","else)"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The * pattern matches any remaining value and is normally placed last.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In nano, what does ^O WriteOut mean?","options":["Press the characters ^ and O","Ctrl+O to save","Alt+O to open","Shift+O to exit"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"^ in nano's shortcuts means Ctrl.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which key returns vi from insert mode to command mode?","answer":["Esc","Escape","esc","escape"],"explanation":"Esc ends text insertion and returns to command mode.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In vi, you need to open an empty line ABOVE the current one and start typing. Which command does this?","options":["o","O","i","a"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"O inserts a line above; o inserts below.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does 2dd do in vi command mode?","options":["Copies two lines","Deletes the current and next lines","Deletes two characters","Moves to line 2"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"dd deletes a line; a numeric prefix repeats the action.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You accidentally changed a file in vi and want to exit while keeping the old version on disk.","options":[":wq",":q!",":w!",":x"],"answer":[1],"explanation":":q! discards unsaved changes.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 vi actions save changes and exit the editor?","options":[":wq","ZZ",":w",":e!"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":":w only saves; :e! reloads the old version.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you replace every old with new throughout a file in vi?","options":[":s/old/new/",":%s/old/new/g","/old/new","%replace old new"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"% selects all lines; g selects all matches within each line.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In if [ \"$age\" -lt 16 ]; then ..., what does -lt mean?","options":["Less than","Less than or equal to","Greater than","The strings match"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-lt means less than, a strict numeric comparison.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"false is followed by echo ok and then echo $?. Whose status is displayed?","options":["false","echo ok","The entire script's future status","The first argument's status"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"$? updates after every command; the successful echo replaced false's status.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 11_ Basic Scripting.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1L_46DJQxlCkCAlgA7baxRM7Qv73r8wcs/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 11 - Basic Scripting.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RK3VgIx7u_1XT95r-PN9UlmKK6zrOXoz/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":12,"title":"Computer Hardware","en":"Understanding Computer Hardware","intro":"This chapter connects physical computer components with how Linux presents them. Distinguish processing, memory, persistent storage, and device connections, then select the appropriate diagnostic command.","sections":[{"title":"Motherboard, processor, and architecture","body":"The motherboard connects the CPU, RAM, and devices. The CPU executes instructions. Multiple processors form a multiprocessor system; multiple cores in one chip form a multicore processor. In the course terminology, x86 refers to a 32-bit architecture and x86_64 to a 64-bit architecture. x86_64 processors also support a compatible 32-bit mode; application support also depends on the operating system. arch displays the architecture, lscpu provides CPU details, and /proc/cpuinfo lists characteristics and flags."},{"title":"RAM and swap","body":"RAM stores running programs and data; its contents are lost when power is removed. Swap is disk space used to temporarily hold memory pages. It helps when RAM is scarce but does not match RAM's speed. A 32-bit address space contains 2^32 addresses, or 4 GiB with byte addressing; actual platform capabilities depend on architectural extensions. free -m and free -g show memory at the corresponding scales, including swap."},{"title":"Buses and peripherals","body":"A bus connects components. PCI/PCIe are used for internal controllers and expansion cards; USB serves many external devices. lspci lists PCI devices, lspci -k shows associated drivers, and lsusb lists USB devices. Hot-plug means connecting while the system is running; cold-plug requires powering it off. Hot-plug support for storage does not remove the need to unmount its file system correctly."},{"title":"Disks, partitions, and devices","body":"An HDD uses rotating platters; an SSD uses memory without moving parts. SSDs are generally faster and quieter; capacity and cost depend on the model. A partition is a logical portion of a drive. MBR limits the number of partitions and addressable capacity; GPT removes key MBR limitations. The course covers fdisk/cfdisk/sfdisk and gdisk/cgdisk/sgdisk, plus parted and gparted. fdisk -l only lists partition tables. Device files are in /dev: in the sd* naming scheme, /dev/sda is a disk and /dev/sda1 is one of its partitions; /dev/sdb2 is the second partition of the second disk."},{"title":"Optical media, drivers, and modules","body":"CD, DVD, and Blu-ray are removable optical media; the specific drive and disc determine whether writing is possible. Automatic mount points are often in /media, while temporary manual mounts use /mnt. umount detaches a file system from the directory tree. A driver enables the OS to communicate with a device; it may be built into the kernel or loaded as a module. lsmod shows loaded modules, their sizes, and dependencies. Hardware compatibility depends on system support."},{"title":"Video and power","body":"A video controller produces the image for a monitor; supported modes depend on both devices and the driver. The course names VGA, DVI, HDMI, DisplayPort, and Mini DisplayPort; VGA carries an analog video signal. The power supply converts alternating current to direct current at the required voltages. It affects the entire system's reliability; a laptop battery can bridge a short interruption in external power."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"lscpu","description":"Architecture, operating modes, cores, and CPU characteristics."},{"cmd":"head -n 20 /proc/cpuinfo","description":"The beginning of detailed processor information."},{"cmd":"free -m","description":"RAM and swap usage."},{"cmd":"lspci -k","description":"PCI devices and kernel drivers."},{"cmd":"lsusb","description":"Connected USB devices."},{"cmd":"lsmod","description":"Loaded modules."},{"cmd":"sudo fdisk -l","description":"Inspect disks and partitions without interactive editing."}],"lab":[{"task":"Compare the CPU architecture and supported operating modes using two commands.","answer":"arch\nlscpu","explanation":"The first gives a short architecture name; the second distinguishes the current architecture from supported operating modes."},{"task":"Check RAM and swap, then find PCI devices, USB devices, and loaded drivers.","answer":"free -m\nlspci -k\nlsusb\nlsmod","explanation":"These represent different levels: memory resources, device buses, and software modules."},{"task":"Display partition tables without entering editing mode.","answer":"sudo fdisk -l","explanation":"The -l option lists information and exits; device numbers depend on the computer."}],"pitfalls":["A device appearing in lspci does not prove that its driver supports every feature.","Swap is on storage; it does not add physical RAM modules.","The number in /dev/sda1 identifies a partition; /dev/sda identifies the whole device.","In a virtual machine, some hardware is represented by virtual devices, so the output differs from the course example."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Which computer component directly executes program instructions?","options":["RAM","CPU","Power supply","Optical disc"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The processor executes code; RAM stores the instructions and data in use.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Four processing cores are located in one processor chip. What is this called?","options":["Multicore","Four separate disks","Hot-plug","Swap"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Multicore means multiple cores within one processor.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you quickly find the current system's architecture?","options":["arch","lsusb","who","route"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"arch prints the hardware architecture name.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does x86_64 mean in the course context?","options":["64 processors","A 64-bit architecture in the x86 family","64 GB of installed RAM","A file system name"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The number describes the architecture's bit width, not the device count.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command shows both the architecture and CPU core information?","options":["lscpu","ls -cpu","free -c","uname --ram"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"lscpu summarizes processor information.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which /proc file contains processor details and flags?","answer":["/proc/cpuinfo"],"explanation":"The flags list supported CPU capabilities.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which resource normally loses its contents when power is removed?","options":["HDD","SSD","RAM","DVD-ROM"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"RAM is volatile memory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The system moves rarely used memory pages to disk. What is the area used for this called?","options":["Swap","PCI","ROM","DNS"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Swap provides disk-backed support for memory management.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does free -m display?","options":["Only free HDD space","RAM and swap in MiB","A list of USB drives","The motherboard temperature"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"free reports memory; -m changes the output units.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How many byte addresses does a 32-bit address space contain?","options":["2^16","2^32","32 million","64 billion"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"32 bits provide 2^32 combinations, corresponding to about 4 GiB with byte addressing.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How do you check whether an internal PCI Ethernet controller is detected?","options":["lsusb","lspci","free","passwd"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A PCI controller is listed by lspci.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You need a PCI device's name and the kernel driver in use. What should you choose?","options":["lspci -k","lscpu -k","lsmod -p","lsusb -d"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"lspci's -k option adds driver and module information.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A USB mouse is connected. Which command should you use to find it in the device list?","options":["lsusb","fdisk -l","arch","top"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"lsusb lists devices on the USB bus.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is hot-plug?","options":["Connecting while the system is running","CPU overheating","Accelerated formatting","Connecting only after shutdown"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Hot-plug contrasts with cold-plug.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why should a USB disk still be unmounted before removal?","options":["USB does not support hot-plug","File system writes may still be pending","Otherwise the CPU will change","Unmounting necessarily deletes files"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Physical hot-plug support does not guarantee that all data writes have finished.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does /dev/sdb2 represent in the course's naming scheme?","options":["The second partition of the second sd disk","The second disk without partitions","The second USB port","The second driver version"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"b identifies the second disk; 2 is the partition number.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How does an SSD's construction differ from a conventional HDD?","options":["It has more rotating platters","It stores data without moving mechanical parts","It works only through USB","It loses data without power"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"An SSD uses nonvolatile memory instead of rotating platters.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Select 2 properties of GPT compared with the traditional MBR partitioning discussed in the course.","options":["Support for disks beyond MBR's typical size limit","A more flexible number of partitions","A requirement for rotating platters","An inability to use Linux"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"GPT extends partitioning capabilities; the storage device type does not determine the partition table format.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"You only need to inspect partitions. Which command matches the lab?","options":["fdisk -l","fdisk --delete-all","mkfs /dev/sda","rm /dev/sda"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-l lists the tables and exits.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does lsmod list?","options":["All available driver packages","Only USB devices","Loaded kernel modules","Disk partitions"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"A loaded module is a kernel software component, not the device itself.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which directory normally holds automatic mount points for removable media?","options":["/etc","/media","/bin","/proc"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"In the FHS, /media is intended for removable-media mount points.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is a power supply's main function?","options":["Convert alternating current to direct current at the required voltages","Assign IP addresses","Store processes","Display graphics"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Computer components require DC supply voltages.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which of the listed video interfaces is traditionally analog?","options":["HDMI","DisplayPort","VGA","Mini DisplayPort"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"VGA is an analog interface, unlike the listed digital interfaces.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A device appears in the bus listing, but some features do not work. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The device automatically became a partition","A suitable driver or required driver capability is missing","The username must be changed","Swap must be deleted"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Detecting hardware and supporting its features through a driver are different things.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 12_ Understanding Computer Hardware.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZnlXt2k5zx5o-pGuosfPD-xpXnDTpfnU/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 12 - Understanding Computer Hardware.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jA993zOCGh1VFz72JtP3jubQnwUMhm7u/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":13,"title":"Processes, Memory, and Directories","en":"Where Data is Stored","intro":"In Linux, it is useful to distinguish data stored on disk from the current state of the kernel. This chapter covers process monitoring, job control, logs, and the locations of system files.","sections":[{"title":"The kernel, /proc, and /sys","body":"The kernel manages processes, memory, filesystems, networking, and drivers; applications access it through system calls. /proc and /sys are virtual filesystems whose contents are supplied by the kernel. /proc/PID describes the process with that ID; /proc/1 describes the first process. /proc/cmdline contains kernel boot parameters, /proc/meminfo memory information, /proc/modules modules, and /proc/cpuinfo CPU information. /dev contains device files. Values in /proc/sys can change the behavior of the running kernel; sysctl configuration is used to preserve settings across reboots."},{"title":"Processes and monitoring","body":"A process is a running instance of a program. Its PID identifies it, while its PPID identifies its parent. The first process has PID 1 (init or systemd). pstree and ps --forest display parent-child relationships. ps provides a snapshot; ps -e, ps -ef, and ps aux broaden the selection to the whole system, ps -u user filters by user, ps -o selects columns, and --sort specifies sorting. top refreshes the list and displays CPU use, memory, and load averages; q quits, k sends a signal, and r changes the nice value. A higher nice value generally means lower priority."},{"title":"Jobs and signals","body":"& starts a command in the background, and jobs lists jobs in the current shell. A job number such as %1 is local to the shell; a PID identifies a process. fg %1 brings a job to the foreground, Ctrl+Z suspends it, and bg %1 resumes it in the background. Ctrl+C normally interrupts the foreground command. By default, kill PID sends SIGTERM (15), allowing graceful termination; SIGKILL (9) cannot be caught or ignored. killall and pkill select processes by name. /dev/null accepts and discards data written to it."},{"title":"Memory and load","body":"The kernel separates protected kernel space from user space; virtual addresses give processes their own view of memory. free displays RAM, caches, and swap; -s 10 repeats the output every 10 seconds. Cache can be reclaimed for applications, so a low free value alone does not prove a memory shortage. Load averages over 1, 5, and 15 minutes describe the average number of running, runnable, and uninterruptible tasks, rather than CPU percentages; interpretation depends on the number of CPU cores and I/O waits."},{"title":"Logs and kernel messages","body":"Logs help investigate errors and login attempts. Their main location is /var/log. Depending on the system, files include syslog/messages, auth.log/secure, cron, maillog, boot.log, and Xorg.0.log. syslogd, klogd, rsyslogd, and journald represent different logging mechanisms. journalctl reads the systemd journal; dmesg reads the kernel ring buffer. Log rotation renames or archives an old log and starts a new one. Not all logs are text: use last for wtmp and lastb for btmp; file helps identify the format."},{"title":"FHS and data locations","body":"The FHS describes a standard directory tree. / is its root, /boot contains boot files, /etc system configuration, /home user home directories, /root the root user's home, /bin and /sbin essential commands, and /lib and /lib64 libraries. /usr contains programs and shared data, /usr/local locally installed software, /opt additional applications, and /srv service data. /media and /mnt provide mount points. /var holds changing data in log, cache, spool, and lib; /tmp holds temporary files, while /var/tmp holds temporary files preserved across reboots. Actual distributions may merge some directories using links."},{"title":"Packages and libraries","body":"A program's files are distributed among bin, lib, share, etc, and var. dpkg -L package and rpm -ql package list files belonging to an installed package. Shared libraries often use the .so extension; versions for different architectures can coexist. Documentation is found in /usr/share/doc, man, and info; personal configuration is often in hidden files in the home directory. The FHS also distinguishes variable/static and shareable/unshareable data. Growth in /var can fill a shared root partition."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"ps -eo pid,ppid,user,%mem,cmd --sort=-%mem","description":"Snapshot of all processes, with the largest memory consumers first."},{"cmd":"top","description":"Continuously updated monitoring."},{"cmd":"sleep 60 &","description":"A practice background job."},{"cmd":"dmesg | grep -i usb","description":"Find USB-related kernel messages."},{"cmd":"tail -n 5 /var/log/auth.log","description":"Latest authentication events on systems using this log."}],"lab":[{"task":"View the boot parameters and information about process 1.","answer":"cat /proc/cmdline\nps -p 1\ncat /proc/1/cmdline","explanation":"The top-level cmdline describes the kernel; /proc/1/cmdline describes the first process."},{"task":"Start a practice sleep job, bring it to the foreground, suspend it, and resume it in the background.","answer":"sleep 60 &\njobs\nfg %1\nCtrl+Z\nbg %1","explanation":"%1 applies if the new job was assigned number 1; jobs shows its actual number."},{"task":"Terminate your practice background job with the default signal and check the job list.","answer":"kill %1\njobs","explanation":"Ordinary kill sends SIGTERM; use the job number from your own shell."},{"task":"In top, find the process using the most CPU, then quit.","answer":"top → check %CPU and COMMAND → q","explanation":"This is a live view; PIDs and values need not match the lab screenshot."},{"task":"Compare a process snapshot with login history.","answer":"ps -e\nls /var/log\nlast","explanation":"Processes show current state, while the log shows recorded history."}],"pitfalls":["Ctrl+Z suspends a process; it does not terminate it.","SIGKILL prevents the program from performing its normal cleanup.","A PID and a job number are different identifiers.","A load average of 1 does not universally mean 100% CPU usage.","ps -ef | grep name may also show the grep process itself."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Where can you find information about the process with PID 742?","options":["/etc/742","/proc/742","/var/742","/dev/cpu742"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Numeric directories in /proc correspond to PIDs.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"What PID does the first user-space process receive after the kernel boots?","answer":["1"],"explanation":"Init or systemd serves as process 1.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does PPID stand for?","options":["Process priority","Parent process ID","Memory percentage","Port number"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"PPID identifies the parent that created the process.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How does top differ from an ordinary ps command?","options":["top only edits processes","top refreshes its display; ps provides a snapshot","ps does not show PIDs","top only works with disks"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"top is designed for ongoing monitoring.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you list all processes with detailed information?","options":["ps -ef","ps -home","jobs -all-users","who -p"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-e selects all processes, and -f requests the full format.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does pstree display?","options":["The directory tree","A tree of parent-child process relationships","Only error streams","Swap size"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"pstree visualizes the process hierarchy.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you run sleep 60 while keeping the shell available for other commands?","options":["sleep 60 &","sleep 60 >","sleep 60 <","sleep 60 !"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"& runs the command in the background.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The shell prints [2] 918. What do these numbers mean?","options":["PID 2 and GID 918","Job 2 and PID 918","Two threads and 918 bytes","Port 2 and address 918"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The bracketed number is the current shell's job ID; the second number is a PID.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which keys suspend a foreground process without terminating it?","options":["Ctrl+C","Ctrl+Z","Ctrl+D","Ctrl+L"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Ctrl+Z normally sends a job-stop signal.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you resume suspended job 2 in the background?","options":["fg %2","bg %2","kill -9 2","jobs 2"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"bg resumes a stopped job without taking over the terminal.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"What is the signal number for SIGTERM?","answer":["15"],"explanation":"It is the default signal sent by an ordinary kill command.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is SIGTERM usually preferred to SIGKILL for a normal shutdown?","options":["SIGTERM raises priority","The program can handle it and release resources","SIGKILL always saves data","SIGTERM only works with files"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"SIGKILL cannot be caught and does not let a termination handler run.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What happens to stdout when redirected with > /dev/null?","options":["It is written to a log","It is discarded","It becomes stderr","The command necessarily stops"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"/dev/null accepts data without storing it.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which ps sort key puts the largest %mem values first?","options":["--sort=%mem","--sort=-%mem","--sort=+%mem","--sort=pid"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A minus sign before a field name requests descending order.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A process's nice value increases from 0 to 10. How does its CPU priority normally change?","options":["It increases","It decreases","The process becomes root","Its PID changes"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A more accommodating process has a higher nice value and a lower priority.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What do the three load-average numbers represent?","options":["RAM, swap, and disk","Average load over 1, 5, and 15 minutes","Percentages for three users","The three latest PIDs"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"top, uptime, and /proc/loadavg use these time intervals.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement about /proc is correct?","options":["It is only an archive of terminated processes","It is a virtual filesystem exposing current kernel data","Every /proc file can be saved with a text editor","It is stored only on USB"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The kernel supplies its contents; ordinary disk sizes do not describe these pseudo-files.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tool specifically reads the systemd journal?","options":["journalctl","catctl","sysctl","logmod"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"journalctl interprets the journal maintained by journald.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Where are system logs normally stored?","options":["/var/log","/usr/bin","/home/bin","/etc/skel"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"/var/log is the standard log directory.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 correct directory → purpose pairs.","options":["/etc → system configuration","/usr/local → locally installed software","/proc → an archive of ISO images","/boot → users' personal documents"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The FHS separates configuration, programs, boot files, and current system data.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you find USB-related kernel messages without case sensitivity?","options":["dmesg | grep -i usb","last | grep -x usb","free -m usb","lsmod > usb"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"dmesg reads the kernel ring buffer, and grep filters its lines.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does log rotation do?","options":["Deletes all events every time","Moves older entries into separate files and continues with a new log","Converts PIDs to UIDs","Disables logging"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Historical data is separated from the current log and may be compressed.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which path is the root user's usual home directory?","options":["/","/root","/home","/usr/root"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The root of the directory tree, /, differs from the administrator's home directory, /root.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you list the files belonging to the installed Debian package nano?","options":["dpkg -L nano","rpm -install nano","cat /proc/nano","nano -files"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"dpkg -L lists the files of an installed package.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command displays RAM information repeatedly every 10 seconds?","options":["free -s 10","free -n 10","top -r 10","ps --memory=10"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The free option -s sets the refresh interval.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does the .so extension used by many libraries stand for?","options":["System owner","Shared object","Socket only","Sorted output"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A shared library contains code used by multiple programs.","difficulty":"basic"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 13_ Where Data is Stored.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_5Yix7ppNw1JMCfP62eVPCHq3MEyJJU1/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 13 - Where Data is Stored.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/15HSqZP1CLUeNoE6ezvudLgCQrKazv-zJ/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":14,"title":"Network Configuration","en":"Network Configuration","intro":"Check networking in layers: the interface and address, routing, name resolution, and then the availability of a specific service. A successful ping and an accessible service are different checks.","sections":[{"title":"Key terminology","body":"A host is a networked device; a network is a group of communicating hosts; a server provides a service, and a client accesses it. A router or gateway connects networks. Data travels in packets according to protocol rules; TCP/IP is a protocol suite. Ethernet usually describes wired networking, while Wi-Fi is wireless. An IP address identifies an interface, and a mask specifies the network portion of the address. A hostname is a human-readable name; a URL also includes a protocol and may include a path."},{"title":"IPv4, IPv6, and address assignment","body":"IPv4 uses 32 bits: four decimal numbers from 0 to 255 separated by dots. IPv6 uses 128 bits, written as hexadecimal groups separated by colons; :: can compress one sequence of zero groups. Loopback addresses are 127.0.0.1 and ::1, on interface lo. DHCP automatically supplies an address and other settings; static settings are configured manually. NAT lets multiple internal hosts share a gateway's external address. Moving between IPv4 and IPv6 requires compatible devices and software."},{"title":"Configuration files used in the course","body":"Interface configuration paths depend on the distribution. The course's older CentOS example uses /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-eth0: IPADDR, PREFIX, GATEWAY, and DNS1 specify static settings, while BOOTPROTO=dhcp enables DHCP. IPv6 examples use IPV6INIT, IPV6ADDR, IPV6_DEFAULTGW, and DHCPV6C. ifdown/ifup operate on one interface; restarting the whole network affects all connections. These examples are not universal across Linux systems."},{"title":"Name resolution","body":"/etc/hosts contains local address-to-name mappings; /etc/resolv.conf contains nameserver entries and DNS settings, including search/domain; /etc/nsswitch.conf controls the order of sources. hosts: files dns means check the local hosts file first, then DNS if no result is found. A successful local match is normally not replaced by DNS merely because the address is incorrect. dig and host query DNS; dig -x performs a reverse lookup by IP, and host -t selects the record type. An FQDN includes both the host name and domain."},{"title":"Checking interfaces and routes","body":"ifconfig displays interfaces; ip addr show is a modern alternative. route/route -n and ip route show display routes; the default route is used when no more specific route matches. ping -c 4 host limits the number of ICMP requests; without a limit, Linux ping normally continues until Ctrl+C. Missing replies may indicate ICMP filtering. If access by IP works but access by name fails, check name resolution."},{"title":"Connections, ports, and SSH","body":"netstat -tln and ss -tln show listening TCP sockets numerically: -t means TCP, -l listening, and -n no name resolution. netstat -r shows routes and -i interface statistics; ss -s gives a socket summary. In ss output, Local Address is the local address/port, Peer Address is the remote endpoint, and Recv-Q/Send-Q are data queues. SSH normally uses TCP port 22; /etc/services maps service names to port numbers. ssh user@host opens an encrypted remote session, and exit returns to the local session. Verify the server key fingerprint on first connection; known_hosts stores trusted keys. An unexpected key change requires investigation."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"ip addr show","description":"Interface addresses."},{"cmd":"ip route show","description":"The routing table."},{"cmd":"ping -c 4 localhost","description":"Four loopback checks."},{"cmd":"dig example.com","description":"A DNS lookup by name."},{"cmd":"dig -x 192.168.1.2","description":"A reverse DNS lookup."},{"cmd":"ss -tln","description":"Listening TCP ports in numeric form."},{"cmd":"ssh student@host","description":"Log in to a remote host as student."}],"lab":[{"task":"Find your IP addresses and default route.","answer":"ip addr show\nip route show","explanation":"An interface address and a gateway address serve different roles; their actual values depend on the environment."},{"task":"Inspect the local localhost mapping and send exactly four ping requests.","answer":"grep '127.0.0.1' /etc/hosts\nping -c 4 localhost","explanation":"A loopback test checks the local network stack but does not prove Internet access."},{"task":"Inspect the configured DNS server, then perform forward and reverse lookups for the lab server.","answer":"cat /etc/resolv.conf\ndig cserver.example.com\ndig -x 192.168.1.2","explanation":"The lab addresses work only in its configured network; ANSWER contains the result, and SERVER identifies the responding DNS server."},{"task":"Check whether SSH is listening on TCP port 22.","answer":"ss -tln","explanation":"Look for :22 under Local Address. A listening socket indicates a local service; external access also depends on networking and filtering."}],"pitfalls":["ping localhost does not test Internet access.","A failed ping does not prove that a web service or SSH is unavailable.","dig queries DNS, while system name resolution may use /etc/hosts through NSS.","A port listening on 127.0.0.1 does not imply that another computer can access the service.","Do not automatically ignore an SSH warning about a changed host key."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"What is a host that provides web pages to other devices called?","options":["Client","Server","Mask","Packet"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A server provides a service to clients.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What connects different IP networks?","options":["A text editor","Router/gateway","A filename","RAM"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A router forwards packets between networks.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"How many bits are in an IPv6 address?","answer":["128"],"explanation":"IPv4 contains 32 bits; IPv6 contains 128.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which address has a valid IPv4 format?","options":["192.168.1.25","192.168.400.1","192:168:1:25","192.168.1"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"It must contain four octets, each from 0 to 255.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the purpose of DHCP?","options":["Encrypting files","Automatically assigning network settings","Deleting DNS records","Listing processes"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"DHCP can supply an IP address, mask, gateway, and DNS settings.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the purpose of DNS?","options":["Mapping domain names to network addresses","Assigning rwx permissions","Partitioning a disk","Measuring RAM"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"DNS stores names and associated records.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In which file would you look for a nameserver entry?","options":["/etc/passwd","/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/services","/etc/skel"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"resolv.conf specifies DNS servers and resolver settings.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does hosts: files dns mean in /etc/nsswitch.conf?","options":["Always use both answers simultaneously","Check /etc/hosts first, then DNS if necessary","Use network files only","Check DNS first, then /etc/hosts"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The order of sources matters.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A name is found in /etc/hosts, but its address is wrong; NSS uses files dns. What usually happens?","options":["DNS necessarily corrects the error","The local match is used without consulting DNS","The system deletes the entry","A random address is assigned"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"NSS sees a successful name lookup even if the address does not lead to the intended machine.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command is a modern replacement for viewing ifconfig address information?","options":["ip addr show","ip user show","ip port remove","ip disk show"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"ip addr show displays interface addresses.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you display the routing table?","options":["ip route show","ip passwd show","dig -r","who -route"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The route object of the ip command handles routes.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"When is the default route used?","options":["Only for localhost","For a destination with no more specific matching route","For any file in /etc","For every packet regardless of the routing table"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The most specific matching route is selected; default is the fallback.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does ping -c 4 localhost check?","options":["Four requests to the local host","Four Internet DNS servers","The first four SSH ports","Disk speed over 4 seconds"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-c limits the request count, and localhost refers to this machine.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Ping by IP succeeds, but the name cannot be resolved. What should you check first?","options":["GPT partitioning","DNS/NSS and local name mappings","Permissions on /bin/bash","The number of CPU cores"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The network path to the IP works; the likely problem is translating the name into an address.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Ping receives no reply. Which conclusion is justified?","options":["The host is definitely powered off","ICMP may be blocked; further checks are needed","All services are definitely unavailable","The IP address must be changed immediately"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A remote system may serve TCP connections without answering ICMP.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you query the DNS name associated with IP 192.168.1.2?","options":["dig -x 192.168.1.2","dig -n 192.168.1.2","ping -d 192.168.1.2","route -x 192.168.1.2"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-x enables a reverse lookup, usually for a PTR record.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In cserver.example.com, which part is the domain in the course example?","options":["cserver","example.com","com.cserver","192.168.1.2"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The full name combines the host name cserver with the domain example.com.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does -n mean in netstat -tln?","options":["New connections","Numeric addresses and ports without name resolution","IPv6 only","Disable networking"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-n lets you see 22 instead of the service name ssh.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 correct meanings of the options in netstat -tln.","options":["-t: TCP","-l: listening","-t: login time","-l: local files"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"The command restricts output to listening TCP sockets.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which TCP port does SSH normally use?","answer":["22"],"explanation":"The standard SSH service listens on port 22; an administrator can change it.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you connect to host using the student account?","options":["ssh host/student","ssh student@host","su student@host","host ssh student"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The user@host syntax specifies the remote user.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"SSH warns that a host key has unexpectedly changed. What should you do?","options":["Immediately delete all of known_hosts","Check whether the server was reinstalled and verify the new key","Disable key checking permanently","Send your password to any responding host"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A legitimate key replacement or server impersonation could cause the warning.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which technology lets private hosts share a gateway's external IPv4 address?","options":["NAT","RAM","GECOS","FHS"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"NAT translates network addresses as packets pass through the gateway.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does Peer Address mean in ss output?","options":["The address of the remote endpoint","The home directory","The local UID","The partition table"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Local Address refers to this machine, while Peer refers to the other endpoint.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does /etc/services contain names such as ssh?","options":["To map service names to ports and protocols","To store SSH passwords","To list current users","To select the default gateway"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The file describes known mappings; it does not start services itself.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 14_ Network Configuration.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rShChsxbrWhGrnua8_bpR0SIsSMT36A0/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 14 - Network Configuration.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1c7mEk-N2fYUfimZ1T54nQeBJAm_4sJqM/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":15,"title":"System and User Security","en":"System and User Security","intro":"Process permissions depend on the account and its groups. For the exam, learn to read user databases, distinguish current sessions from login history, and understand the difference between su and sudo.","sections":[{"title":"The administrator, su, and sudo","body":"UID 0 determines root privileges. Regular users receive only the access granted to them; system accounts are used by services and are normally not intended for interactive login. su - user opens another user's login shell with that user's environment; omitting the name implies root. A regular user normally needs the target account's password. sudo command runs a command permitted by policy, as root by default, and normally requests the calling user's password. Execution is logged. The interval before another password prompt depends on configuration, so the 5- and 15-minute values in the materials are not universal."},{"title":"/etc/passwd","body":"Each line represents an account; seven fields are separated by colons: name:x:UID:GID:comment:home_directory:shell. x indicates that the password hash is stored in shadow. The system uses UIDs and GIDs for identification; names provide a convenient representation. The GID here specifies the primary group. /root is normally root's home; a regular user often receives /home/name. /usr/sbin/nologin or /bin/false in the shell field restricts interactive login."},{"title":"/etc/shadow and expiration","body":"Access to shadow is restricted. It stores the name, password hash or marker, last-change day, min, max, warn, inactive, expire, and a reserved field. Last-change and expire dates are expressed as days since January 1, 1970. Min specifies the minimum interval between changes, max the password lifetime, and warn the number of warning days. Inactive is the interval after password expiration before disabling the account; expire is a separate account expiration date. ! and * are not ordinary passwords and may disable password authentication. A hash is neither plaintext nor a reversibly encrypted password."},{"title":"System accounts and groups","body":"Services use separate accounts with limited privileges; low UIDs are often reserved for them, but exact ranges depend on the distribution. /etc/group has the format name:x:GID:user_list. The final field lists supplementary memberships; the primary group comes from passwd, so a user need not also appear in the group list. /etc/gshadow stores protected group information. The same name can exist as both a username and a group name."},{"title":"Viewing identity","body":"id displays the current user's UID, primary GID, and all groups; id name displays the specified account. id -g prints the primary GID, while id -G prints all GIDs. grep '^name:' /etc/passwd searches for a local entry; getent passwd name queries configured NSS databases, including network directories. getent group name displays a group. man 5 passwd documents the file format rather than the password-changing command."},{"title":"Sessions and history","body":"who shows currently recorded login sessions, terminals, login times, and login sources; who -b shows boot time, and who -r shows the runlevel. w adds uptime, load, idle time, and the current command in WHAT. TTY means terminal; pts is a pseudo-terminal, which can be used locally or remotely. last reads wtmp history, including ended sessions and reboots; still logged in means a recorded session has not ended. An account's existence does not prove that it has ever logged in."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"su -","description":"A root login shell, if this authentication method is allowed."},{"cmd":"sudo head -n 3 /etc/shadow","description":"A privileged-read example from the lab."},{"cmd":"getent passwd student","description":"A user record from configured sources."},{"cmd":"id student","description":"The user's UID and groups."},{"cmd":"who","description":"Currently recorded logins."},{"cmd":"w","description":"Current sessions and their activity."},{"cmd":"last student","description":"The user's recorded login history."}],"lab":[{"task":"In the lab VM, compare your identity before su -, after it, and after exit.","answer":"id\nsu -\nid\nexit\nid","explanation":"The administrative shell should have UID 0; exit returns to the previous shell."},{"task":"Interpret the entry ana:x:1100:1200:Student:/home/ana:/bin/bash.","answer":"Name ana, UID 1100, primary GID 1200, comment Student, home /home/ana, shell /bin/bash.","explanation":"x is a placeholder for the hash in shadow, not the user's password."},{"task":"Check current sessions and login history; compare the three commands.","answer":"who\nw\nlast","explanation":"who and w show current sessions; last shows recorded history. Output depends on the environment."},{"task":"Look up an account through the system database and read the format documentation.","answer":"getent passwd student\nman 5 passwd","explanation":"getent may include network records; manual section 5 describes the file format."}],"pitfalls":["sudo normally asks for your password; su asks for the target user's password.","sudo requires policy authorization: knowing a password alone is not enough.","Do not confuse /etc/passwd with the passwd command.","A name missing from the final field of /etc/group may still have that group as its primary group.","A pts terminal alone does not prove a remote login."],"questions":[{"type":"text","prompt":"Which UID determines root administrative privileges?","answer":["0"],"explanation":"Privileges are associated with UID 0, not merely with the name root.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Whose password does sudo normally request from an authorized regular user?","options":["Always root's","The calling user's own password","The file owner's","Any group member's"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"In a standard configuration, sudo verifies the calling user's identity.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A regular user runs su - bob. Whose password is normally required?","options":["The current user's","bob's","Always root's","The bob group's password"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"su authenticates the switch to the target account.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the purpose of - with su?","options":["To forbid commands","To start a login shell with the target user's environment","To view passwords","To delete a session"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A login shell loads the target user's settings.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you return from a shell started by su to the previous user?","options":["exit","reboot","userdel","logout --all"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"exit terminates the current nested shell.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which file contains general local account records?","options":["/etc/passwd","/etc/services","/etc/resolv.conf","/var/log/wtmp"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"passwd stores the UID, GID, home directory, shell, and other account information.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In ali:x:1050:2000:Student:/home/ali:/bin/bash, what is the primary GID?","options":["1050","2000","0","Not specified"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The GID is the fourth field, after the UID.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does x normally mean in the second field of /etc/passwd?","options":["The user is executable","The hash is stored in /etc/shadow","The password is literally x","The account has been deleted"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"It is a placeholder, not the actual password.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"How many fields does a standard /etc/passwd entry contain?","answer":["7"],"explanation":"Colons separate the name, password/placeholder, UID, GID, comment, home, and shell.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Where is a local user's password hash normally stored?","options":["/etc/shadow","/etc/hosts","/usr/bin/passwd","/home/passwords"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"shadow restricts access to hashes and password-aging settings.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In shadow, min=5 and max=30. What does this mean?","options":["The password contains 5–30 characters","At least 5 days between changes; a 30-day password lifetime","A UID from 5 to 30","A 30-minute lockout after 5 failed attempts"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Both fields describe time, rather than password length or attempt counts.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How does expire differ from max in shadow?","options":["Expire is the account expiration date; max is the password lifetime","They are identical fields","Expire specifies password length","Max specifies the number of groups"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Account expiration and password expiration are separate mechanisms.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does a password hash represent?","options":["A plaintext password in another encoding","The result of a one-way transformation","The owner's IP address","The shell name"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A hash is used to verify an entered password; it is not reversible encryption.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why are services assigned system accounts?","options":["To make every service root","To restrict access to the resources they need","To hide all processes from ps","To disable UIDs"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A separate identity limits the consequences of a compromised service.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which shell normally indicates that an account is not intended for ordinary interactive login?","options":["/bin/bash","/bin/sh","/usr/sbin/nologin","/usr/bin/less"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"nologin refuses interactive login.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In research:x:1500:ana,bob, what does 1500 mean?","options":["Password","ana's UID","The GID of research","The number of users"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The third field of group is its numeric group ID.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"ana is absent from the research list in /etc/group, but her passwd GID equals the GID of research. Is she a member?","options":["Yes, it is her primary group","No, the entry must be duplicated","Only after sudo","It is impossible to determine even with the GID known"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Primary membership is defined in passwd; the group list primarily records supplementary memberships.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you display all GIDs for the current user?","options":["id -g","id -G","who -G","last -g"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Uppercase -G prints all groups; lowercase -g prints the primary group.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is getent passwd user more useful than grepping /etc/passwd for network accounts?","options":["getent can query configured network sources","getent automatically creates the user","grep modifies the file","getent always displays the password"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"NSS can combine local databases with a network directory.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command opens documentation specifically for the /etc/passwd file format?","options":["man 5 passwd","man 1 shadow","passwd --doc","help /etc/passwd"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Manual section 5 covers file formats.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 commands are intended to show currently recorded user login sessions?","options":["who","w","lastb","groupadd"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"who and w show current logins; lastb shows failed-login history.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An administrator wants to see what a user is doing now, including the WHAT column. Which command fits?","options":["w","who -b","getent group","last -r"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"w adds the current command and activity information to the session list.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you view recorded history of completed logins?","options":["last","id","groups","whoami"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"last reads wtmp and displays past sessions.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does who -b display?","options":["The user's byte count","The latest boot time","Passwords of locked accounts","A list of Bash scripts"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The -b option (boot time) displays the latest system boot time, not user login history.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement about sudo is correct?","options":["Anyone who knows their own password can become root","Access is determined by policy, and actions may be logged","It always starts a permanent root shell","Every Linux system caches the password for exactly 5 minutes"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Policy and configuration determine permissions and reauthentication requirements.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 15 - System and User Security.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1diGXwA3pPUMutAsx6Issbuv7U1TgNlvk/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 15_ System and User Security.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1hcFNFaRvQnPaJe4m66zwTCtmYQEvyBvd/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":16,"title":"Creating Users and Groups","en":"Creating Users and Groups","intro":"Account management requires distinguishing account creation, password assignment, group membership, and file ownership. Renaming usually does not change numeric ownership, while changing a UID or GID requires attention to existing files.","sections":[{"title":"Planning and groups","body":"It is convenient to create groups before users. groupadd team creates a group, -g specifies a GID, and -r selects the system-group range. Distribution settings determine valid ranges; the old 500 and 1000 boundaries in the course are examples. groupmod -n new old changes the name; -g changes the GID. Renaming preserves the GID and file associations; changing a GID does not automatically rewrite the GID of every file. groupdel removes a group but normally refuses to delete an existing user's primary group. Files belonging to a deleted group may remain with a numeric GID."},{"title":"Defaults","body":"useradd -D shows settings from /etc/default/useradd: GROUP, HOME, INACTIVE, EXPIRE, SHELL, SKEL, and CREATE_MAIL_SPOOL. Here HOME is the base directory for new home directories; SKEL is the source of initial files. /etc/login.defs contains UID/GID ranges, password-aging parameters, CREATE_HOME, UMASK, USERGROUPS_ENAB, and other defaults. A user private group (UPG) usually has the same name as its user. Defaults apply when creating new accounts and do not necessarily change existing ones."},{"title":"Creating a user","body":"The required useradd argument is the username. -u specifies the UID, -g an existing primary group, -G a comma-separated list of supplementary groups, -c a comment, and -s the shell. -m creates a home directory, while -M prevents its creation; -b specifies the base directory, -d the full home path, and -k an alternative skeleton together with -m. /etc/skel is copied into the new home, and the user owns the copies. The command updates passwd/shadow and membership records; successful creation does not imply that a working password exists."},{"title":"Passwords and aging","body":"passwd without an argument changes your own password; an administrator uses passwd user to set another account's initial or replacement password. chage -l lists aging settings; -m sets minimum days, -M maximum days, -W warning days, -I the inactivity period after expiration, -E the account expiration date, and -d the last-change date. Password input in the terminal is normally not echoed. System policy determines password complexity and change rules; numeric course examples are not universal requirements."},{"title":"Modifying an account","body":"usermod shares many useradd options: -c, -d, -g, -G, -s, -u, -e, and -f. -l changes the login name. usermod -aG team user adds a supplementary group; -G without -a replaces the entire supplementary-group list. An active session normally does not acquire new groups until the user logs in again. -L locks the password, and -U unlocks it; this does not guarantee that every login method, such as SSH keys, is blocked. Changing a UID may require correcting ownership of files outside the home directory."},{"title":"Deletion and verification","body":"userdel user deletes the account while retaining home files. userdel -r user also removes the home directory and mail spool, but not every file owned by that user throughout the disk. Remaining files may become orphaned: their UID/GID no longer maps to a name. Check results with getent, id, passwd/group, and last history. For portability, use short names with lowercase letters, digits, _, and -, avoiding an initial digit and a trailing hyphen."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"sudo groupadd research","description":"Create a group."},{"cmd":"sudo useradd -m -G research -c 'Linux Student' student","description":"Create a user with a home directory and a supplementary group."},{"cmd":"sudo passwd student","description":"Set an initial password."},{"cmd":"sudo usermod -aG research student","description":"Add a group while retaining other memberships."},{"cmd":"chage -l student","description":"View password/account aging settings if access is permitted."}],"lab":[{"task":"In the lab VM, create the research group and verify its entry.","answer":"sudo groupadd research\ngetent group research","explanation":"The group must exist before you specify it with -G for a new user."},{"task":"Create student with a home directory and the supplementary group research, then set a password.","answer":"sudo useradd -m -G research -c 'Linux Student' student\nsudo passwd student\nid student","explanation":"useradd creates the account; passwd sets its password separately. The actual primary group depends on UPG settings."},{"task":"Add the existing sysadmin user to research without losing previous group memberships.","answer":"sudo usermod -aG research sysadmin\ngetent group research","explanation":"-a means append. A new login is normally required for membership to affect the current working session."},{"task":"Compare userdel student with userdel -r student without running them against an account you need.","answer":"The first command deletes the account record. The second also deletes the home directory and mail spool.","explanation":"Files elsewhere can remain; deletion does not transfer their ownership."}],"pitfalls":["usermod -G without -a can remove previous supplementary memberships.","useradd without -m does not create a home directory on every system.","Renaming a group and changing its GID are different operations.","Password locking with -L does not delete files or block every login method.","userdel -r does not automatically find and delete all of a user's files throughout the system."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"Why is it usually convenient to create groups before users?","options":["useradd cannot create users","The groups specified with -g and -G will already exist","It always assigns UID 0","To delete /etc/group"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Required memberships can then be assigned when creating the account.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which command creates group team with GID 2500?","options":["groupadd -g 2500 team","groupmod -u 2500 team","useradd -G 2500 team","groupadd -n 2500 team"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The groupadd option -g sets the numeric group ID.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does groupadd -r service mean?","options":["Recursively delete a group","Create a system group","Rename root","Assign read-only access"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-r creates a group using the system-group range.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you rename sales to clerks while retaining its GID?","options":["groupmod -n clerks sales","groupmod -g clerks sales","groupadd clerks sales","usermod -l sales clerks"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-n supplies the new name; the final argument identifies the existing group.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"After a group's GID changes, files with the old GID display a number. Why?","options":["The files were destroyed","The old GID no longer maps to a group name","The files became links","The UTF-8 format changed"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Files store numeric GIDs; groupmod does not rewrite them across the entire disk.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"When will groupdel normally refuse to delete a group?","options":["It is the primary group of at least one user","Its name contains more than one character","It has a GID above 1000","It is empty"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"You must first change the user's primary group.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which directory normally serves as the skeleton for new home directories?","answer":["/etc/skel"],"explanation":"Its contents are copied when the home directory is created.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does useradd -D show when no modifying options are supplied?","options":["A list of deleted users","Defaults for new accounts","All current passwords","The process tree"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-D displays defaults when used without options that change them.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which useradd option explicitly requests creation of a home directory?","options":["-M","-m","-r","-G"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Lowercase -m creates the home; uppercase -M prevents creation.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In useradd -m -g users -G research,sales ana, which group is primary?","options":["research","sales","users","All three are primary"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"-g specifies one primary group; -G lists supplementary groups.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is the purpose of useradd -c 'Aida Student' aida?","options":["Set a password","Populate the comment/GECOS field","Select a cache directory","Enable encryption"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-c sets the comment, often the user's full name.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A new user needs /bin/bash as the shell. Which useradd option is required?","options":["-s /bin/bash","-b /bin/bash","-k /bin/bash","-p /bin/bash"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-s specifies the login-shell path.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you use /etc/skel-dev as an alternative skeleton when creating a home directory?","options":["useradd -m -k /etc/skel-dev dev","useradd -s /etc/skel-dev dev","useradd -g /etc/skel-dev dev","useradd -D /etc/skel-dev dev"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-k specifies a skeleton directory and is used together with -m.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An account was created successfully, but no password has been set. Which command should the administrator run?","options":["passwd student","passwordadd student","su --new-password","id -p student"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"With sufficient privileges, passwd user sets an initial password.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does usermod -aG research ana do?","options":["Replaces all groups with research","Adds research to ana's supplementary groups","Changes the primary group","Creates research"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-a appends to the -G list without removing previous supplementary memberships.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Ana belonged to sales and audit. The administrator runs usermod -G research ana. What changes?","options":["research is added and everything else stays","The supplementary list becomes research only","The primary group becomes audit","The UID becomes the GID of research"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Without -a, -G replaces the supplementary-group list.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why might an open terminal lack the permissions of a newly added group?","options":["Changes always require reinstallation","A session's groups are normally established at login","getent never updates","Groups do not affect permissions"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Logging in again creates processes with the updated groups.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you display a user's password-aging settings?","options":["chage -l user","chmod -l user","passwd -r user","groupmod -l user"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The chage option -l lists aging parameters.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you limit a password's lifetime to 60 days?","options":["chage -M 60 user","chage -m 60 user","chage -W 60 user","chage -l 60 user"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Uppercase -M sets the maximum; lowercase -m sets the minimum interval between changes.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 statements about userdel -r user are correct?","options":["It deletes the user's home directory","It deletes the user's mail spool","It always deletes every file the user owns throughout the system","It keeps the passwd entry active"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Files elsewhere may retain the previous UID/GID.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does usermod -l new old change?","options":["The login name","The UID necessarily becomes 0","Permissions of all files","Only the password"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Renaming preserves numeric identity unless the UID is changed separately.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which statement most accurately describes usermod -L student?","options":["It deletes the home directory","It locks the password while retaining the account and files","It changes all groups","It guarantees that every authentication method is blocked"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Password locking is not equivalent to blocking access through all other mechanisms.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does CREATE_MAIL_SPOOL=yes mean in the course's useradd settings?","options":["Create local mail storage for the new user","Start an SMTP server automatically","Send the password by email","Delete old messages"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"A spool stores local messages; it does not configure an external mail server.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which name best follows the course's portability recommendations?","options":["research_team","9team","team-","Team Name"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Start with a lowercase letter or _, and avoid spaces and a trailing hyphen.","difficulty":"applied"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 16_ Creating Users and Groups.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1fYHpiWBLb0WTpnHh2cp1Yab1J-m2ab4n/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 16 - Creating Users and Groups.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vPCtO-WSZczjfzD6HM2dliG26HrMMEp9/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":17,"title":"Ownership and Permissions","en":"Ownership and Permissions","intro":"Work through permission problems in order: identify the user and their groups, check access through every directory in the path, then select the permission class for the object itself. This prevents most mistakes in exam scenarios.","sections":[{"title":"Owner and group","body":"A file stores its owner's UID and its group's GID. Normally, a new file belongs to its creator and takes the process's current primary group; setgid directories, covered next, are an important exception. id and groups show memberships. newgrp team starts a new shell with a different current group; exit returns to the previous shell. usermod -g permanently changes an account's primary group. chgrp changes a file's group: a regular owner may select a group they belong to, while root has broader choices. chown user:group changes owner and group; transferring ownership to another user requires an administrator. -R applies a change throughout a directory tree."},{"title":"Reading ls output and file types","body":"In ls -l output, the first character indicates the type: - regular file, d directory, l symbolic link, b block device, c character device, p named pipe, and s socket. The next nine positions are rwx for owner, group, and others. They are followed by link count, owner, group, size, timestamp, and name. ls -ld dir shows the directory itself; ls -la also includes hidden names; stat shows detailed metadata and the numeric mode."},{"title":"What r, w, and x mean","body":"For a regular file, r permits reading its contents, w modifying them, and x executing it directly as a program. For a directory, r permits listing names, x traversing it and accessing known names within it, and w together with x creating, deleting, and renaming entries. Reading a file requires x on every directory in its path and r on the file; r is not required on every directory. Deletion normally depends on the parent directory's permissions rather than w on the file; the sticky bit and other mechanisms may impose additional restrictions."},{"title":"Selecting an access class","body":"For a basic permission check without ACLs, matching the owner UID selects only u permissions. Otherwise, membership in the file's group selects g permissions; if neither matches, o permissions apply. Classes are not combined, and more permissive others bits do not help an owner with u=---. Each directory in the path is checked separately. If the user's group membership is unknown, there may be insufficient information to answer. Unless stated otherwise, problems assume a regular user without root privileges or additional ACLs."},{"title":"Changing the mode","body":"chmod is available to the owner and administrator. Symbolic notation combines u/g/o/a, an operation +/−/=, and r/w/x: g+w adds group write, o-r removes others read, and u=rx replaces the owner permissions with exactly r-x. Numeric notation uses r=4, w=2, x=1: 7 rwx, 6 rw-, 5 r-x, 4 r--, 3 -wx, 2 -w-, 1 --x, and 0 ---. The three digits are ordered owner–group–others. chmod 640 produces rw-r-----."},{"title":"Creation permissions: umask","body":"umask removes specified bits from the mode requested by the creating program; it is a bitmask, not ordinary numeric subtraction. Typical new files start with 666 and directories with 777, then the mask bits are removed: 027 gives file mode 640 and directory mode 750; 022 gives 644 and 755; 077 gives 600 and 700. A mask never adds x to a regular file and does not modify existing files. umask displays or sets the current shell's value; child processes inherit it. For a persistent setting, use the appropriate shell configuration file; the course uses ~/.bashrc."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"ls -ld project","description":"Permissions of the directory itself."},{"cmd":"stat note.txt","description":"Ownership, mode, and metadata."},{"cmd":"chmod u+x,go-w task.sh","description":"Add owner execute and remove group/others write."},{"cmd":"chmod 640 note.txt","description":"Owner can read/write, group can read, and others have no permissions."},{"cmd":"chgrp research note.txt","description":"Change the file's group to research if permitted."},{"cmd":"sudo chown student:research note.txt","description":"Set the owner and group."},{"cmd":"umask 027","description":"Typical new files receive 640 and directories 750."}],"lab":[{"task":"Create practice private/public directories and separate files; compare the directory permissions with those of their contents.","answer":"mkdir priv-dir pub-dir\ntouch priv-dir/priv-file pub-dir/pub-file\nls -ld priv-dir pub-dir\nls -l priv-dir pub-dir","explanation":"-d changes the object being listed: without it, ls displays entries inside the directory."},{"task":"Remove group and others access to priv-file while preserving owner permissions.","answer":"chmod go= priv-dir/priv-file\nls -l priv-dir/priv-file","explanation":"An empty value after = removes all permissions for the selected classes."},{"task":"test.sh contains date. Allow the owner to run it directly, then set mode 775 and verify it.","answer":"chmod u+x test.sh\n./test.sh\nchmod 775 test.sh\nstat test.sh","explanation":"775 corresponds to rwxrwxr-x; running a script also depends on access to the interpreter and all directories in the path."},{"task":"In the lab VM, change the owner of one directory and recursively change the group of another.","answer":"sudo chown root:root pub-dir\nsudo chgrp -R users priv-dir","explanation":"The first command changes only pub-dir itself; -R in the second includes the directory and everything beneath it."}],"pitfalls":["Exactly one of u/g/o is selected; their permissions are not combined.","Directory r permits listing names; x is required to traverse the path to a file.","A file without w can still be removed from a writable, searchable directory without the sticky bit.","umask removes bits: with mask 013, a typical regular file receives 664, not the arithmetic result of 666−013.","Write permission on a file alone does not grant the right to change its mode with chmod."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"In mode -rwxr-x---, what permissions do others have?","options":["rwx","r-x","---","rw-"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The final triplet applies to others.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"text","prompt":"What is the numeric value of the read permission?","answer":["4"],"explanation":"r=4, w=2, and x=1; add the values within each triplet.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which numeric mode corresponds to rw-r-----?","options":["640","604","740","460"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Owner: 4+2=6; group: 4; others: 0.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How is mode 751 written symbolically?","options":["rwxr-x--x","rwxr-xr--","rw-r-x--x","rwxrw---x"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"7=rwx, 5=r-x, and 1=--x.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file had mode 644. What is its mode after chmod g+w file?","options":["654","664","744","646"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Group write adds w=2, changing the group digit from 4 to 6.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file had mode 777. After chmod u=rx file, what is its mode?","options":["577","757","775","555"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"= replaces only the owner permissions with r-x=5.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you view permissions of project itself instead of listing its files?","options":["ls -ld project","ls -l project/*","cat project","ls -a /"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-d makes ls display the directory argument itself.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which first character in ls -l output indicates a character device?","options":["b","c","s","l"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"c means character device; b means block device.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does r without x permit on a directory, assuming its parent path is accessible?","options":["Reading every file's contents","Listing names, but not necessarily obtaining metadata for the files inside","Creating a new file","Executing any file inside"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Reading directory entries and traversing them are separate operations.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which permission is required on every directory in the path to read a known file inside?","options":["x","w","Only r","None"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"x means search/traverse: permission to use the directory as a path component.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Ana has r on /data/report but lacks x on /data. Can she read the file through this path?","options":["Yes, file permissions override directory permissions","No, the path cannot be traversed","Yes, if the file is smaller than 1 KB","Only if its extension is .txt"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The ability to traverse parent directories is checked before accessing the file.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A user has x but not r on a directory; a file with a known name inside is readable. Can the user read it?","options":["No, directory r is always required","Yes, if the other directories in the path are also accessible","Only root can","Only through ls"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"For a known path, directory x and file r suffice; listing names remains unavailable.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Without a sticky bit or ACLs, and with an accessible parent path, which directory permissions are needed to delete a known entry inside?","options":["r and x","w and x","Only r on the file","x on the file"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Deletion changes the directory; w permits changing entries and x permits accessing them by name.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Ana owns a file with mode 047 and belongs to its group. Can she read it as a regular user?","options":["Yes, through group permissions","Yes, through others permissions","No: u=--- applies","Yes, by combining all permissions"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"The owner uses only the u class; there is no fallback to group or others.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A non-owner belongs to the group of a file with mode 604. Can they read it without extra permissions?","options":["Yes, through others permissions","No, the selected g triplet is ---","Yes, everyone has 4","Only after running chmod themselves"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A group match selects g permissions even if others are allowed to read.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Who can run chmod on a regular file under the basic permission model?","options":["Anyone with r","The owner or administrator","Any member of the file's group","Only the directory's creator"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Permission to write contents is not the same as permission to change the mode.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A regular owner wants to run chgrp team file. What is required?","options":["They must belong to team","The file must be executable","team must have GID 0","All others must have w"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The owner may select a group from their own memberships.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does newgrp research do?","options":["Creates research","Starts a shell with a different current primary group","Rewrites the groups of all files","Changes the UID to root"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"New files in ordinary directories use the new shell's current group.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can an administrator permanently change ana's primary group?","options":["usermod -g research ana","newgrp -p ana research","chmod g=research ana","id -g research ana"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"usermod changes the account record; newgrp changes the context of a new shell.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does chown bob:team file do?","options":["Sets mode 777","Changes the owner to bob and group to team","Adds bob to team","Creates a hard link"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The user is specified before the colon and the group after it.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A typical new regular file starts with 666 and umask 027. What is the resulting mode?","options":["640","750","639","666"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Removing g-w and all o bits from 666 leaves 640.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A typical new directory starts with 777 and umask 027. What is the resulting mode?","options":["640","750","770","027"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Directories initially include x, so the resulting mode is 750.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A new file starts with mode 666 and umask 013. What mode results?","options":["653","664","654","663"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Mask g=1 cannot remove an absent x; o=3 removes w and x, leaving r. The bitwise result is 664.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What happens to an existing file with mode 666 after umask 077?","options":["Its mode becomes 600","Its mode stays 666","It is deleted","Its mode becomes 700"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"umask applies during creation and does not modify existing objects.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Choose 2 results of umask 022 with the usual creation modes.","options":["New file: 644","New directory: 755","New file: 755","Existing files automatically become 644"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Files start with 666 and directories with 777; the mask removes g/o write.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which tool shows a mode in both symbolic and numeric form?","options":["stat","who","route","getent"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"stat displays extended metadata, including Access/mode.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does -R mean in chgrp -R team project?","options":["Only readable files","Recursively change the group of the directory and its contents","Only the root user","Undo the previous command"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"-R traverses the entire tree under project.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Bob's membership in team is unknown. /data is owned by sue, belongs to team, and has mode 750. Is this enough to determine whether bob can enter /data?","options":["Yes, he can always enter","Yes, he will always be denied","No, bob's membership in team is needed","Only the directory extension is needed"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Membership gives g=r-x; otherwise o=--- applies.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 17_ Ownership and Permissions.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_ANr_fIYp9wTrnoT_YH3l2FiTTYDngho/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 17 - Ownership and Permissions.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sh43Y4pT7Fl3VE3bOSamwLbTRTR2dESk/view?usp=drivesdk"}]},{"id":18,"title":"Special Permissions and Links","en":"Special Directories and Files","intro":"Special bits extend rwx: setuid/setgid affect a running program's identity, directory setgid controls the group of new objects, and the sticky bit restricts deletion in a shared directory. Hard links and symlinks provide additional names in different ways.","sections":[{"title":"Setuid","body":"On a binary executable, setuid allows the process to run with the program owner's effective UID. The course example is passwd, which must update protected /etc/shadow without granting users general access to it. In the owner execute position, s means setuid together with x, while S means setuid without that x. chmod u+s adds the bit and u-s removes it; the special octal digit for setuid is 4, as in 4755. The bit does not necessarily mean root: the result depends on the program owner. Linux normally does not honor setuid on shell scripts."},{"title":"Setgid on files and directories","body":"On a binary executable, setgid sets the effective group to the file's group; an example is wall with group tty. On a directory, it causes new files to inherit the directory's group, and new subdirectories also inherit setgid. This helps team members collaborate even when they have different primary groups. The bit does not guarantee group write permission: the creation mode and umask still matter. s/S appears in the group execute position. chmod g+s adds the bit and g-s removes it; its special digit is 2, as in 2770."},{"title":"The sticky bit and temporary directories","body":"In a shared writable directory, the sticky bit restricts deletion and renaming: normally, only the file owner, directory owner, or a privileged process may do so. Basic directory permissions are still required. It does not prevent reading or modifying file contents when ordinary permissions allow those operations. /tmp and /var/tmp normally have mode 1777. In the others execute position, t means sticky plus x, while T means sticky without o+x. chmod +t adds the bit and -t removes it; its special digit is 1. /var/tmp is intended for temporary data that persists across reboots."},{"title":"Inodes and hard links","body":"An inode stores metadata: UID/GID, mode, timestamps, size, and pointers to data; names are stored in directory entries. ln target alias creates another entry pointing to the same inode. Both names are equal; content and mode changes affect the same object. ls -i shows inode numbers; ls -li also shows hard-link counts. Removing one name decreases the count, while other names continue to work. Hard links cannot cross filesystem boundaries and are not normally created for directories."},{"title":"Symbolic links","body":"ln -s target alias creates a separate object with its own inode that stores a target path. ls -l shows type l and an arrow to the target. A symlink can refer to a directory, another filesystem, or even a path that does not yet exist. It does not increase the target's hard-link count. If the target path disappears, the link remains, but access through it cannot find the data. A relative target is resolved relative to the directory containing the link itself. Access depends on target permissions and path traversal; a symlink's own ordinary rwx bits do not independently grant access on Linux."},{"title":"Comparison and selection","body":"A hard link provides another equal name within one filesystem: deleting the original name does not break the other link. A symbolic link is convenient for directories and links across filesystems, and ls -l displays its target. A copy creates independent data; a link provides another name or path to an object. Comparing inode numbers is meaningful only within the same filesystem."}],"commands":[{"cmd":"ls -ld /tmp /var/tmp","description":"Identify the sticky bit on temporary directories."},{"cmd":"ls -l /usr/bin/passwd /usr/bin/wall","description":"Examples of setuid and setgid from the lab."},{"cmd":"chmod g+s project","description":"Make new objects inherit the directory's group."},{"cmd":"chmod +t shared","description":"Restrict deletion in a shared directory."},{"cmd":"ln source hardlink","description":"Create a second name for the same inode."},{"cmd":"ln -s source softlink","description":"Create a link storing the path source."},{"cmd":"ls -li source hardlink softlink","description":"Compare inodes, types, and link counts."}],"lab":[{"task":"Identify where sticky, setuid, and setgid appear on the lab system.","answer":"ls -ld /tmp /var/tmp\nls -l /usr/bin/passwd /usr/bin/wall","explanation":"t occupies the others position; passwd's s is in the user position and wall's s in the course example is in the group position. Actual system settings may differ."},{"task":"In a separate practice directory, create source and two hard links. Compare their inodes.","answer":"echo data > source\nln source hardlink\nln hardlink second\nls -li source hardlink second","explanation":"The three names normally share one inode with link count 3; none is more primary than the others."},{"task":"Remove only the practice link second. What changes?","answer":"rm second\nls -li source hardlink","explanation":"The link count becomes 2; the contents remain accessible through both names."},{"task":"Create a symbolic link to source and another to /proc.","answer":"ln -s source softlink\nln -s /proc proc-link\nls -li source softlink proc-link","explanation":"A symlink has its own inode and may refer to a directory on another filesystem."}],"pitfalls":["Setuid gives a program the file owner's effective UID, which is not necessarily root.","Directory setgid passes on the group but does not automatically grant g+w to new files.","The sticky bit restricts deletion/renaming, not reading or writing file contents.","A hard link is not an independent backup copy.","A symbolic link does not protect against the target path disappearing."],"questions":[{"type":"single","prompt":"In the basic scenario, what does setuid change on a binary executable?","options":["The process's effective UID becomes the program owner's UID","The username in passwd","Every user's primary group","The UID of every file in the directory"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The running process's identity changes, not the entire account.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why can a regular user update their password through the setuid program passwd?","options":["shadow is writable by everyone","The program performs a controlled update with its root owner's privileges","Passwords are stored only in RAM","sudo starts automatically inside the shell"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The privileged program checks whether the change is allowed instead of opening shadow to arbitrary writes.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"An executable is owned by service and has setuid. Must it run with effective UID 0?","options":["Yes, setuid always means root","No, its effective UID corresponds to service","Only if the file is named passwd","No, setuid means group"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"The special bit uses the file owner's identity.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does lowercase s in the user execute position mean?","options":["Setuid and user x are both set","Only setuid is set; user x is absent","Only sticky is set","The file must be a socket"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Lowercase s combines the special bit with ordinary execute permission.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does uppercase S in the group execute position mean?","options":["Setgid is set, but g+x is absent","Sticky is set","The group has all permissions","The file is compressed"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Letter case indicates whether ordinary execute is present in that position.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"text","prompt":"Which special octal digit represents setuid?","answer":["4"],"explanation":"A leading special digit 4 sets bit 4000.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you add setgid to a directory while retaining its ordinary permissions?","options":["chmod g+s project","chmod u+s project","chmod o+t project","chown +g project"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"g+s adds setgid without replacing the other rwx bits.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why does wall have setgid tty in the course example?","options":["To write messages to terminals belonging to tty","To change root's password","To run every user command as root","To delete /tmp"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The process's effective group gains access to terminal devices.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"project has group team and setgid. Ana, whose primary group is sales, creates a file inside it. What group does the new file receive?","options":["sales","team","root","None"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Directory setgid causes the directory's group to be inherited.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does a new subdirectory normally inherit inside a setgid directory?","options":["Only the owner name root","The parent group and setgid","All members' passwords","Only the sticky bit"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Inheritance preserves the group organization of the tree.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file in a setgid directory receives group team and mode 640. Can another team member edit it?","options":["Yes, setgid always grants w","No, the group has only r","Yes, the directory overrides the file mode","Only if its name ends in .txt"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Group inheritance does not add a missing write permission.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does the leading 1 mean in directory mode 1777?","options":["Setuid","Setgid","Sticky bit","Read-only"],"answer":[2],"explanation":"Special values are 4 for setuid, 2 for setgid, and 1 for sticky.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which problem does the sticky bit address in a shared directory?","options":["Preventing reads of other users' files","Restricting deletion of other users' entries","Automatic encryption","Faster writes"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"It restricts deletion and renaming where users share directory write access.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"In a sticky directory, which 2 regular users may delete a file if the basic permissions are sufficient?","options":["The file owner","The directory owner","Any member of the file's group","Anyone who can read the file"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"Apart from a privileged process, either the file owner or directory owner may delete it.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A directory has mode 1777, and ana's file has mode 666. Bob does not own the directory. Which behavior follows?","options":["Bob cannot delete ana's file, but he can modify its contents because of w","Bob can neither read nor write it","Bob becomes the owner","The file automatically becomes 600"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Sticky controls directory-entry operations; the file's write permission remains in effect.","difficulty":"challenge"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What does t in the final directory-permission position mean?","options":["Sticky and execute for others","Only owner execute","Setuid without execute","A socket"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"t occupies the o+x position; uppercase T indicates that o+x is absent.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Which directory is intended for temporary files that survive reboots?","options":["/var/tmp","/bin","/proc/1","/etc/skel"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"The FHS distinguishes /var/tmp from the shorter-lived /tmp.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"What is stored in a directory entry connecting a filename to an object?","options":["The name and inode number","The owner's plaintext password","A complete copy of all file data","Only the shell path"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"Names are stored in directory entries; an inode stores the object's metadata.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you create hard link alias to source?","options":["ln source alias","ln -s source alias","cp -r alias source","link -d source alias"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"ln without -s creates a hard link.","difficulty":"basic"},{"type":"single","prompt":"source and alias are hard links to one regular file. What do they share?","options":["Only their filename extension","One inode and the same contents","They always have different permissions","Only the link creation date"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Both names refer to the same filesystem object.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A file has link count 1. Two hard links and one symlink are added. What is the target's link count?","options":["2","3","4","1"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"Each hard link increases the count; a symlink does not change it.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"The original filename is removed, but one hard link remains. What happens to the data?","options":["It remains accessible through the remaining name","It disappears immediately","It becomes a directory","Only root can access it"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"All hard-linked names are equal; deleting one does not delete the others.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"multi","prompt":"Which 2 capabilities does a symlink have that an ordinary hard link does not?","options":["Linking to a directory","Crossing to another filesystem","Necessarily sharing the target's inode","Increasing the target's hard-link count"],"answer":[0,1],"explanation":"A symbolic link stores a path, so it is not limited to inodes within one filesystem.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you create symbolic link docs to /usr/share/doc?","options":["ln -s /usr/share/doc docs","ln /usr/share/doc docs","cp /usr/share/doc docs","chmod -s docs"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-s selects a symbolic link.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"A symlink's target is deleted, but the link itself is not. What remains?","options":["A link whose target path cannot be resolved","A complete independent copy","An automatically created hard link","Nothing; the symlink deletes itself"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"This is a dangling or broken link; the link object still exists.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"How can you display inode numbers when listing files?","options":["ls -i","ls -h","ls -t","ls -R"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"-i adds inode numbers; -li also shows detailed information.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"Why can hard links not cross filesystem boundaries?","options":["Each filesystem has its own inode namespace","Because the shell forbids the / character","Because root can never create links","Because of filename case"],"answer":[0],"explanation":"An inode belongs to one particular filesystem.","difficulty":"applied"},{"type":"single","prompt":"In /home/ana, symlink shortcut has target docs/readme. Where is this relative path resolved from?","options":["The calling process's current directory","/home/ana, the directory containing the link","Always /","/proc"],"answer":[1],"explanation":"A relative target is resolved from the directory containing the symlink.","difficulty":"challenge"}],"sources":[{"kind":"lab","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Lab 18_ Special Directories and Files.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1InGHVU44W4tG0PIgryfBwUs5Cp_Lpgok/view?usp=drivesdk"},{"kind":"chapter","title":"NDG Linux Essentials - Chapter 18 - Special Directories and Files.txt","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/13OenzPh5Htugd9wcyjvYVy82oaHmkmYH/view?usp=drivesdk"}]}];
